  (3, 3, '<h2>Divisibility Rules</h2>
<hr>
<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Core Concept</h4>
<p>
Divisibility rules are mental shortcuts that allow you to determine whether a given integer is divisible by another integer without performing long division.
</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Rule for 2:</b> The last digit is even (0, 2, 4, 6, 8).</li>
<li><b>Rule for 3:</b> The sum of all digits is divisible by 3.</li>
<li><b>Rule for 4:</b> The number formed by the last two digits is divisible by 4.</li>
<li><b>Rule for 5:</b> The last digit is 0 or 5.</li>
<li><b>Rule for 6:</b> The number is divisible by both 2 and 3.</li>
<li><b>Rule for 9:</b> The sum of all digits is divisible by 9.</li>
<li><b>Rule for 10:</b> The last digit is 0.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>2. Key Principle</h4>
<p>
Every integer <i>n</i> can be expressed in base-10 expansion. The properties of powers of 10 modulo <i>d</i> determine the divisibility test for divisor <i>d</i>.
</p>
', 1),
  (4, 4, '<h2>Modular Arithmetic</h2>
<hr>
<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Concept of Clock Arithmetic</h4>
<p>
Modular arithmetic is a system of arithmetic for integers where numbers ''wrap around'' upon reaching a certain value, known as the <b>modulus</b>.
</p>
<p>
We write: <code>a ≡ b (mod m)</code> if and only if <i>m</i> divides <code>(a - b)</code>. Equivalently, <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> have the same remainder when divided by <code>m</code>.
</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Fundamental Properties</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Addition:</b> (a + b) mod m = [(a mod m) + (b mod m)] mod m</li>
<li><b>Multiplication:</b> (a × b) mod m = [(a mod m) × (b mod m)] mod m</li>
<li><b>Exponentiation:</b> (a^k) mod m = [(a mod m)^k] mod m</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (5, 5, '<h2>Prime Numbers</h2>
<hr>
<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Definition</h4>
<p>
A <b>prime number</b> is a whole number greater than 1 whose only positive divisors are 1 and itself. A number greater than 1 that is not prime is called a <b>composite number</b>.
</p>
<p><b>Note:</b> The number 1 is neither prime nor composite.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic</h4>
<p>
Every integer greater than 1 either is a prime number itself or can be represented as the product of prime numbers in a way that is unique up to the order of the factors.
</p>
', 1),
  (6, 6, '<h2>Congruence Relations</h2>
<hr>
<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Definition of Congruence</h4>
<p>
Two integers <i>a</i> and <i>b</i> are said to be <b>congruent modulo m</b> (denoted <code>a ≡ b (mod m)</code>) if their difference <code>a - b</code> is an integer multiple of <code>m</code>.
</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Equivalence Relation Properties</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Reflexive:</b> a ≡ a (mod m)</li>
<li><b>Symmetric:</b> If a ≡ b (mod m), then b ≡ a (mod m)</li>
<li><b>Transitive:</b> If a ≡ b (mod m) and b ≡ c (mod m), then a ≡ c (mod m)</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (7, 7, '
<h2>Distance</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> Distance is the total length of the path travelled by an object.</p>

<h3>What is distance?</h3>

<p>
When something moves, it follows a path from one position to another.
The <b>distance</b> tells us the total length of that path.
</p>

<p>
Distance answers:
</p>

<p><b>How far did the object travel?</b></p>

<p>
Distance does not describe the direction of movement. It only measures
the total length travelled.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: A straight journey</h3>

<p>
A student walks 800 m from home to school.
Find the distance travelled.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify what the question gives.</p>

<p>
The student walks 800 m.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify what is being asked.</p>

<p>
The question asks for the distance.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Distance = <b>800 m</b></p>

<h3>Example 2: A journey with two sections</h3>

<p>
A student walks 300 m from home to a shop and then 200 m from the shop
to school. Find the total distance travelled.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the first part.</p>

<p>
Home to shop = 300 m
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the second part.</p>

<p>
Shop to school = 200 m
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Add the lengths of the two parts.</p>

<p>
Distance = 300 + 200
</p>

<p>
Distance = <b>500 m</b>
</p>

<p><b>Answer: 500 m</b></p>

<h3>Example 3: A journey with three sections</h3>

<p>
A cyclist travels 2 km from A to B, 3 km from B to C and 4 km from C
to D. Find the total distance travelled.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> List every section of the path.</p>

<p>
A to B = 2 km
</p>

<p>
B to C = 3 km
</p>

<p>
C to D = 4 km
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Add all sections.</p>

<p>
Distance = 2 + 3 + 4
</p>

<p>
Distance = <b>9 km</b>
</p>

<p><b>Answer: 9 km</b></p>

<h3>Important idea</h3>

<p>
If an object travels along several sections, the total distance is the
sum of the lengths of all the sections travelled.
</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>
Do not count only the final section of a journey. Distance includes
the <b>whole path travelled</b>.
</p>
', 1),
  (8, 8, '
<h2>Displacement</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> Displacement is the straight-line change in position from the starting point to the final point, together with its direction.</p>

<h3>Think about the starting and final positions</h3>

<p>
Suppose a student starts at point A and walks directly to point B.
If B is 500 m east of A, the student''s displacement is:
</p>

<p><b>500 m east</b></p>

<p>
The distance describes the path travelled. Displacement describes the
change from the <b>starting position</b> to the <b>final position</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Straight movement</h3>

<p>
A car moves 80 km east from town A to town B.
Find its displacement.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the starting position.</p>

<p>
The car starts at town A.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the final position.</p>

<p>
The car finishes at town B.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Identify the direction.</p>

<p>
Town B is 80 km east of town A.
</p>

<p><b>Answer: Displacement = 80 km east.</b></p>

<h3>Example 2: Moving west</h3>

<p>
A cyclist moves 3 km west from point P to point Q.
Find the displacement.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The cyclist starts at P.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The cyclist finishes at Q.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Q is 3 km west of P.</p>

<p><b>Answer: Displacement = 3 km west.</b></p>

<h3>Example 3: Returning to the starting point</h3>

<p>
A student walks 2 km east from a starting point and then walks 2 km
west back to the same starting point. Find the displacement.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the starting position.</p>

<p>
The student starts at the original point.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the final position.</p>

<p>
The student returns to the same point.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Compare the starting and final positions.</p>

<p>
There is no change in position.
</p>

<p><b>Answer: Displacement = 0 km.</b></p>

<h3>Important idea</h3>

<p>
An object can travel a large distance and still have zero displacement
if it returns to its starting position.
</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>
Do not give displacement as just a number when direction is required.
Displacement has both a <b>magnitude</b> and a <b>direction</b>.
</p>
', 1),
  (9, 9, '
<h2>Distance and Displacement Compared</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> Distance measures the total path travelled, while displacement measures the change in position from start to finish.</p>

<h3>The key difference</h3>

<p>
Distance asks:
</p>

<p><b>How much path was travelled?</b></p>

<p>
Displacement asks:
</p>

<p><b>How far and in what direction is the final position from the starting position?</b></p>

<h3>Example 1: Moving in one direction</h3>

<p>
A car travels 100 km east without changing direction.
</p>

<p>
The total path travelled is 100 km.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
Distance = <b>100 km</b>
</p>

<p>
Displacement = <b>100 km east</b>
</p>

<p>
In this case, the magnitudes are equal because the car travelled in one
straight direction.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Moving and returning</h3>

<p>
A person walks 4 km east and then 4 km west back to the starting point.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Find the distance.</p>

<p>
Distance = 4 + 4
</p>

<p>
Distance = <b>8 km</b>
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Find the displacement.</p>

<p>
The person finishes at the starting position.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
Displacement = <b>0 km</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Different distances but same displacement direction</h3>

<p>
A student walks 3 km east and then 2 km east.
Find the distance and displacement.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Find the distance.</p>

<p>
Distance = 3 + 2
</p>

<p>
Distance = <b>5 km</b>
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Find the displacement.</p>

<p>
Both movements are east, so the student''s final position is 5 km east
of the starting point.
</p>

<p>
Displacement = <b>5 km east</b>
</p>

<h3>What should you remember?</h3>

<ul>
<li>Distance is based on the <b>whole path</b>.</li>
<li>Displacement is based on the <b>change from start to finish</b>.</li>
<li>Distance has no direction.</li>
<li>Displacement includes direction.</li>
<li>Distance is always greater than or equal to the magnitude of displacement.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (10, 10, '
<h2>Speed</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> Speed is the distance travelled per unit of time.</p>

<h3>What does speed tell us?</h3>

<p>
Speed tells us how quickly distance is being covered.
</p>

<p>
For example, a speed of 60 km/h means that an object covers
60 kilometres in 1 hour if it maintains that speed.
</p>

<p>
The formula is:
</p>

<p><b>Speed = Distance ÷ Time</b></p>

<p>
Using symbols:
</p>

<p><b>s = d / t</b></p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A car travels 120 km in 2 hours. Find its speed.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the distance.</p>

<p>
d = 120 km
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Write the time.</p>

<p>
t = 2 h
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Use the formula.</p>

<p>
s = d / t
</p>

<p>
s = 120 / 2
</p>

<p>
s = <b>60 km/h</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A cyclist travels 45 km in 3 hours. Find the speed.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>

<p>
d = 45 km
</p>

<p>
t = 3 h
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>

<p>
s = 45 / 3
</p>

<p>
s = <b>15 km/h</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A bus travels 240 km in 4 hours. Find its speed.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Distance = 240 km.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Time = 4 h.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>

<p>
s = 240 / 4
</p>

<p>
s = <b>60 km/h</b>
</p>

<h3>Why do we divide?</h3>

<p>
The total distance tells us how far the object travelled.
The total time tells us how long it took.
Dividing distance by time tells us how much distance was covered
for each unit of time.
</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>
Do not multiply distance by time when finding speed.
The correct relationship is:
</p>

<p><b>Speed = Distance ÷ Time</b></p>
