<h2>Average Speed</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> Average speed is the total distance travelled divided by the total time taken.</p>

<h3>The formula</h3>

<p>
When a journey contains different speeds, we cannot simply add the
speeds and divide by the number of speeds.
</p>

<p>
Instead, use:
</p>

<p><b>Average speed = Total distance ÷ Total time</b></p>

<h3>Example 1: Two parts of a journey</h3>

<p>
A car travels 60 km in 1 hour and then 40 km in another 1 hour.
Find its average speed.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Find total distance.</p>

<p>
Total distance = 60 + 40
</p>

<p>
Total distance = <b>100 km</b>
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Find total time.</p>

<p>
Total time = 1 + 1
</p>

<p>
Total time = <b>2 h</b>
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Divide total distance by total time.</p>

<p>
Average speed = 100 / 2
</p>

<p>
Average speed = <b>50 km/h</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Unequal journey times</h3>

<p>
A cyclist travels 30 km in 2 hours and then 20 km in 1 hour.
Find the average speed.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>

<p>
Total distance = 30 + 20 = <b>50 km</b>
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>

<p>
Total time = 2 + 1 = <b>3 h</b>
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>

<p>
Average speed = 50 / 3
</p>

<p>
Average speed = <b>16.67 km/h</b> approximately.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Including a return journey</h3>

<p>
A person travels 80 km from A to B in 2 hours and returns 80 km from B
to A in 4 hours. Find the average speed for the whole journey.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Find total distance.</p>

<p>
Total distance = 80 + 80
</p>

<p>
Total distance = <b>160 km</b>
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Find total time.</p>

<p>
Total time = 2 + 4
</p>

<p>
Total time = <b>6 h</b>
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>

<p>
Average speed = 160 / 6
</p>

<p>
Average speed = <b>26.67 km/h</b> approximately.
</p>

<h3>Important warning</h3>

<p>
Do not automatically calculate average speed by taking the average of
the individual speeds. The correct method is always based on:
</p>

<p><b>Total distance ÷ Total time</b></p>
