<h2>Average Velocity</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> Average velocity is the total displacement divided by the total time taken.</p>

<h3>The formula</h3>

<p>
Average velocity is different from average speed because it uses
<b>displacement</b>, not total distance.
</p>

<p>
The formula is:
</p>

<p><b>Average velocity = Total displacement ÷ Total time</b></p>

<h3>Example 1: Straight movement</h3>

<p>
A car moves 120 km east in 2 hours. Find its average velocity.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Find the displacement.</p>

<p>
Displacement = 120 km east
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Find the total time.</p>

<p>
Time = 2 h
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Divide displacement by time.</p>

<p>
Average velocity = 120 / 2
</p>

<p>
Average velocity = <b>60 km/h east</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Returning to the start</h3>

<p>
A runner completes a journey and returns to the exact starting point
after 2 hours. The total distance travelled is 10 km.
Find the average velocity.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Determine the displacement.</p>

<p>
The runner finishes at the starting point.
</p>

<p>
Displacement = <b>0 km</b>
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Use the formula.</p>

<p>
Average velocity = displacement / time
</p>

<p>
Average velocity = 0 / 2
</p>

<p>
Average velocity = <b>0 km/h</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Movement in the opposite direction</h3>

<p>
A person moves 40 km west in 4 hours. Find the average velocity.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>

<p>
Displacement = 40 km west
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>

<p>
Time = 4 h
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>

<p>
Average velocity = 40 / 4
</p>

<p>
Average velocity = <b>10 km/h west</b>
</p>

<h3>Important difference</h3>

<p>
Average speed uses:
</p>

<p><b>Total distance ÷ total time</b></p>

<p>
Average velocity uses:
</p>

<p><b>Total displacement ÷ total time</b></p>
