<h2>Reading a Distance-Time Graph</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> A distance-time graph shows how an object''s distance changes as time passes.</p>

<h3>What does each axis represent?</h3>

<p>
In a distance-time graph:
</p>

<ul>
<li>The horizontal axis represents <b>time</b>.</li>
<li>The vertical axis represents <b>distance</b>.</li>
</ul>

<p>
Therefore, every point on the graph connects a particular time with a
particular distance.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Reading one point</h3>

<p>
Suppose a distance-time graph contains the point:
</p>

<pre>
(time, distance) = (2 h, 120 km)
</pre>

<p>
The horizontal coordinate is 2 h, so the time is <b>2 hours</b>.
</p>

<p>
The vertical coordinate is 120 km, so the distance is <b>120 km</b>.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> After 2 hours, the object has travelled 120 km.</p>

<h3>Example 2: Reading another point</h3>

<p>
A point on a distance-time graph is:
</p>

<pre>
(4 h, 240 km)
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Read the horizontal coordinate.</p>

<p>
Time = 4 h
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Read the vertical coordinate.</p>

<p>
Distance = 240 km
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> At 4 hours, the object has travelled 240 km.</p>

<h3>Example 3: Comparing two points</h3>

<p>
A distance-time graph contains the points:
</p>

<pre>
A = (2 h, 100 km)
B = (5 h, 250 km)
</pre>

<p>
At point A:
</p>

<p>
Time = 2 h, distance = 100 km.
</p>

<p>
At point B:
</p>

<p>
Time = 5 h, distance = 250 km.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, the object has travelled farther at 5 hours than at
2 hours.
</p>

<h3>Important idea</h3>

<p>
Always check the axis before reading a graph.
The horizontal coordinate gives the <b>time</b>, while the vertical
coordinate gives the <b>distance</b>.
</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>
Do not swap the axes. Reading the distance from the horizontal axis
would give the wrong interpretation.
</p>
