<h2>Gradient of a Distance-Time Graph</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> The gradient of a distance-time graph represents speed.</p>

<h3>Finding the gradient</h3>

<p>
The gradient of a straight line is calculated using:
</p>

<p><b>Gradient = Change in vertical value ÷ Change in horizontal value</b></p>

<p>
On a distance-time graph:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Vertical value = distance</li>
<li>Horizontal value = time</li>
</ul>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Gradient = Change in distance ÷ Change in time</b></p>

<p>
But:
</p>

<p><b>Speed = Distance ÷ Time</b></p>

<p>
Therefore, the gradient of a distance-time graph represents speed.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A straight section of a distance-time graph passes through:
</p>

<pre>
(0 h, 0 km)
(2 h, 100 km)
</pre>

<p>
Find the gradient.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Find the change in distance.</p>

<p>
Change in distance = 100 - 0 = 100 km
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Find the change in time.</p>

<p>
Change in time = 2 - 0 = 2 h
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Divide.</p>

<p>
Gradient = 100 / 2
</p>

<p>
Gradient = <b>50 km/h</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore, the speed is <b>50 km/h</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A graph passes through:
</p>

<pre>
(2 h, 60 km)
(5 h, 180 km)
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>

<p>
Change in distance = 180 - 60 = 120 km
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>

<p>
Change in time = 5 - 2 = 3 h
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>

<p>
Gradient = 120 / 3
</p>

<p>
Gradient = <b>40 km/h</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A graph passes through:
</p>

<pre>
(1 h, 20 km)
(4 h, 140 km)
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>

<p>
Change in distance = 140 - 20 = 120 km
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>

<p>
Change in time = 4 - 1 = 3 h
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>

<p>
Gradient = 120 / 3
</p>

<p>
Gradient = <b>40 km/h</b>
</p>

<h3>Important idea</h3>

<p>
For a distance-time graph:
</p>

<p><b>Gradient = speed</b></p>

<p>
A steeper straight line represents a greater speed.
</p>
