<h2>Finding Time from Distance and Speed</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> When distance and speed are known, time is found using t = d/s.</p>

<h3>Deriving the formula</h3>

<p>
Start with the distance relationship:
</p>

<p><b>d = st</b></p>

<p>
We want time to stand alone.
</p>

<p>
Divide both sides by speed:
</p>

<p>
d / s = st / s
</p>

<p>
The speed cancels from the right-hand side:
</p>

<p>
<b>t = d/s</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A car travels 180 km at 60 km/h. Find the time taken.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the formula.</p>

<p>
t = d/s
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute.</p>

<p>
t = 180 / 60
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate.</p>

<p>
t = <b>3 hours</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A bus travels 240 km at 80 km/h. Find the time taken.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>

<p>
t = d/s
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>

<p>
t = 240 / 80
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>

<p>
t = <b>3 hours</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A train travels 450 km at 90 km/h. Find the time taken.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>

<p>
t = d/s
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>

<p>
t = 450 / 90
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>

<p>
t = <b>5 hours</b>
</p>

<h3>Why do we divide?</h3>

<p>
Speed tells us how much distance is covered in one unit of time.
If we know the total distance, dividing that distance by the rate tells
us how many units of time are needed.
</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>
Do not multiply distance by speed when finding time.
The correct formula is:
</p>

<p><b>Time = Distance ÷ Speed</b></p>
