<h2>Relative Speed in Opposite Directions</h2>

<p>
When two objects move towards each other in <b>opposite directions</b>,
their speeds work together to reduce the distance between them.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Relative speed = speed of first object + speed of second object</b>
</p>

<h3>Why do we add?</h3>

<p>
Suppose one car moves at 60 km/h towards another car moving at 40 km/h.
After one hour, the first car has covered 60 km towards the other car and the
second has covered 40 km towards the first.
</p>

<p>
Together they have reduced the distance by:
</p>

<p>
60 + 40 = 100 km
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Two cars move towards each other at 60 km/h and 40 km/h. Find their relative speed.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>First speed = 60 km/h</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>Second speed = 40 km/h</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>
<p>Relative speed = 60 + 40</p>

<p><b>Answer = 100 km/h</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Two motorcycles travel towards each other at 45 km/h and 35 km/h.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>They move in opposite directions.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>Relative speed = 45 + 35</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>
<p>Relative speed = <b>80 km/h</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A bus travels east at 70 km/h while a car travels west at 50 km/h.
Find their relative speed.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>The vehicles move towards each other.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>Relative speed = 70 + 50</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>
<p>Relative speed = <b>120 km/h</b></p>

<h3>Key Rule</h3>

<p>
<b>Opposite directions → add the speeds.</b>
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Relative Speed in Opposite Directions</h2>

<p>
When two objects move towards each other, both objects reduce the distance
between them.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, their speeds contribute together.
</p>

<p>
<b>Relative speed = v₁ + v₂</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A car travels at 60 km/h towards a bus travelling at 40 km/h in the opposite
direction.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>Car speed = 60 km/h</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>Bus speed = 40 km/h</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>
<p>Relative speed = 60 + 40</p>

<p><b>Answer = 100 km/h</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Two cyclists travel towards each other at 25 km/h and 15 km/h.
</p>

<p>
Relative speed = 25 + 15
</p>

<p>
<b>Relative speed = 40 km/h</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Two vehicles approach one another at 80 km/h and 70 km/h.
</p>

<p>
Relative speed = 80 + 70
</p>

<p>
<b>Relative speed = 150 km/h</b>
</p>

<h3>Why Do We Add?</h3>

<p>
Imagine the distance between the objects.
One object moves towards the other while the second object also moves towards
the first. Both movements reduce the gap.
</p>

<p>
That is why their speeds are added.
</p>
