<h2>Meaning of Linear Programming</h2>

<h3>What is Linear Programming?</h3>

<p>
<b>Linear programming</b> is a mathematical method used to find the
best possible choice when we have limited resources and conditions
that must be satisfied.
</p>

<p>
The word <b>linear</b> means that the mathematical relationships involve
variables only to the first power, such as:
</p>

<p>
x + y ≤ 20
</p>

<p>
2x + 3y ≤ 30
</p>

<p>
The word <b>programming</b> here does not mean computer programming.
It means planning how limited resources should be used.
</p>

<h3>The basic idea</h3>

<p>
Suppose a farmer has limited land and wants to grow two crops.
The farmer cannot grow unlimited amounts of both crops because the
land is limited.
</p>

<p>
Linear programming helps answer questions such as:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>How many of each item should be produced?</li>
  <li>Which combination gives the greatest profit?</li>
  <li>Which combination uses the least cost?</li>
</ul>

<h3>What must be present?</h3>

<p>
A linear programming problem normally has:
</p>

<ol>
  <li>quantities we can choose;</li>
  <li>conditions that limit those quantities;</li>
  <li>an objective such as maximising profit or minimising cost.</li>
</ol>

<p>
At this stage, focus only on the meaning:
<b>we are looking for the best choice while obeying restrictions.</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A bakery makes bread and cakes. The bakery has limited flour.
It wants to decide how many bread loaves and cakes to make.
</p>

<p>
This is a linear programming situation because:
</p>

<p>
1. The quantities of bread and cakes can be chosen.
</p>

<p>
2. Flour is limited.
</p>

<p>
3. The bakery may want to maximise profit.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, linear programming can be used to find the best production
combination.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A farmer grows maize and beans on limited land.
The farmer wants to use the available land to obtain the greatest
possible profit.
</p>

<p>
The decision is how much land should be used for maize and how much
for beans.
</p>

<p>
The limited land creates a restriction.
The profit gives the quantity we want to maximise.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A school wants to buy desks and chairs using a limited budget.
The school wants to purchase as many useful items as possible without
exceeding the money available.
</p>

<p>
The limited budget is a restriction.
The quantities of desks and chairs are choices.
</p>

<h3>Check Your Understanding</h3>

<p>
<b>Question 1:</b> A shop wants to decide how many shirts and trousers
to stock while staying within a limited budget. Is this a possible
linear programming situation?
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Yes.</p>

<p>
<b>Question 2:</b> What makes the situation a linear programming
problem?
</p>

<p>
<b>Answer:</b> There are quantities to choose, restrictions to obey,
and a goal of finding the best choice.
</p>

<p>
<b>Question 3:</b> Does the word programming here mean writing computer
code?
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> No. It means planning the best use of limited resources.</p>
', 1),
