<h2>Decision Variables</h2>

<h3>What is a decision variable?</h3>

<p>
A <b>decision variable</b> represents a quantity whose value we need
to decide.
</p>

<p>
We normally use letters such as <b>x</b> and <b>y</b> to represent
these quantities.
</p>

<p>
The important question is:
</p>

<p>
<b>"What quantity are we trying to decide?"</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A farmer grows maize and beans.
Let:
</p>

<p>
x = hectares of land used for maize
</p>

<p>
y = hectares of land used for beans
</p>

<p>
Here, <b>x</b> and <b>y</b> are decision variables because their values
must be decided.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A shop sells shirts and trousers.
Let:
</p>

<p>
x = number of shirts
</p>

<p>
y = number of trousers
</p>

<p>
The decision variables are therefore <b>x</b> and <b>y</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A bakery produces bread loaves and cakes.
Let:
</p>

<p>
x = number of bread loaves produced
</p>

<p>
y = number of cakes produced
</p>

<p>
Again, x and y are decision variables.
</p>

<h3>Important rule</h3>

<p>
Always state what the variable represents before using it.
</p>

<p>
Writing only:
</p>

<p>
x = 20
</p>

<p>
does not tell us what 20 represents.
</p>

<p>
Writing:
</p>

<p>
x = number of shirts
</p>

<p>
makes the meaning clear.
</p>
', 1),
  (99, 99, '
<h2>Understanding Constraints</h2>

<h3>What is a constraint?</h3>

<p>
A <b>constraint</b> is a condition or restriction that limits the
values our decision variables can take.
</p>

<p>
In linear programming, we cannot simply choose any values we want.
The choices must obey the available resources or other conditions.
</p>

<h3>Think of a constraint as a boundary</h3>

<p>
Suppose a farmer has only 10 hectares of land.
</p>

<p>
If:
</p>

<p>
x = hectares used for maize
</p>

<p>
y = hectares used for beans
</p>

<p>
then the total land used cannot be more than 10 hectares.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
x + y ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
This is a constraint.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A farmer has at most 20 hectares of land for maize and beans.
</p>

<p>
Let x be hectares of maize and y be hectares of beans.
</p>

<p>
The total land used is:
</p>

<p>
x + y
</p>

<p>
Because at most 20 hectares are available:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 20</b>
</p>

<p>
This inequality is the land constraint.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A student has KSh 500 to spend on two items.
Let x and y represent the amounts spent on the two items.
</p>

<p>
The total amount spent cannot exceed KSh 500.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 500</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A workshop can produce at most 100 items per week.
Let x and y represent two types of items.
</p>

<p>
The total production is:
</p>

<p>
x + y
</p>

<p>
At most 100 items can be produced:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 100</b>
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
A constraint tells us which choices are allowed and which choices
are not allowed.
</p>
