<h2>Writing a Constraint from a Statement</h2>

<h3>The skill</h3>

<p>
A linear programming problem often gives us a condition in words.
We must translate that condition into a mathematical inequality.
</p>

<p>
The important steps are:
</p>

<ol>
  <li>Identify the quantities.</li>
  <li>Combine the quantities according to the statement.</li>
  <li>Identify the restriction word.</li>
  <li>Write the inequality.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A farmer has <b>30 hectares at most</b> for maize and beans.
Let:
</p>

<p>
x = hectares of maize
</p>

<p>
y = hectares of beans
</p>

<p>
The total land used is:
</p>

<p>
x + y
</p>

<p>
"At most 30" means the total cannot be greater than 30.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 30</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A workshop has <b>50 hours available</b> for two activities.
If x and y are the hours used for the activities, the total hours
cannot exceed 50.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 50</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A school has <b>at least 200 students</b> in two groups.
Let x and y represent the numbers of students in the two groups.
</p>

<p>
The total number is:
</p>

<p>
x + y
</p>

<p>
"At least 200" means the total cannot be below 200.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≥ 200</b>
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Do not choose the inequality symbol before understanding the words.
The wording determines the mathematical relationship.
</p>
', 1),
  (101, 101, '
<h2>Understanding ≤</h2>

<h3>What does ≤ mean?</h3>

<p>
The symbol:
</p>

<p>
<b>≤</b>
</p>

<p>
means <b>less than or equal to</b>.
</p>

<p>
It combines two ideas:
</p>

<p>
<b>less than</b> OR <b>equal to</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
x ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
This allows:
</p>

<p>
x = 10
</p>

<p>
because x can be equal to 10.
</p>

<p>
It also allows:
</p>

<p>
x = 7
</p>

<p>
because 7 is less than 10.
</p>

<p>
But it does not allow:
</p>

<p>
x = 12
</p>

<p>
because 12 is greater than 10.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Check whether x = 8 satisfies:
</p>

<p>
x ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
Substitute x = 8:
</p>

<p>
8 ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
This is true.
</p>

<p>
Therefore x = 8 is allowed.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Check x = 10 in:
</p>

<p>
x ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
Substitute:
</p>

<p>
10 ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
This is true because equality is allowed.
</p>

<p>
Therefore x = 10 is allowed.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Check x = 13 in:
</p>

<p>
x ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
Substitute:
</p>

<p>
13 ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
This is false.
</p>

<p>
Therefore x = 13 is not allowed.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
The equality part matters.
A value exactly on the limit is allowed when the condition is ≤.
</p>
', 1),
  (102, 102, '
<h2>Understanding ≥</h2>

<h3>What does ≥ mean?</h3>

<p>
The symbol:
</p>

<p>
<b>≥</b>
</p>

<p>
means <b>greater than or equal to</b>.
</p>

<p>
It allows values that are greater than the limit and also the limit
itself.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
x ≥ 20
</p>

<p>
This allows:
</p>

<p>
x = 20
</p>

<p>
and values greater than 20, such as:
</p>

<p>
x = 25
</p>

<p>
But x = 15 is not allowed.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Check x = 25 in:
</p>

<p>
x ≥ 20
</p>

<p>
Substitute:
</p>

<p>
25 ≥ 20
</p>

<p>
This is true.
</p>

<p>
Therefore x = 25 is allowed.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Check x = 20:
</p>

<p>
20 ≥ 20
</p>

<p>
This is true because equality is included.
</p>

<p>
Therefore x = 20 is allowed.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Check x = 17:
</p>

<p>
17 ≥ 20
</p>

<p>
This is false.
</p>

<p>
Therefore x = 17 is not allowed.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
The symbol ≥ means:
</p>

<p>
<b>the value cannot be below the limit.</b>
</p>
', 1),
  (103, 103, '
<h2>Non-Negativity Constraints</h2>

<h3>Why do we need x ≥ 0 and y ≥ 0?</h3>

<p>
In many linear programming problems, x and y represent quantities
such as:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>number of products;</li>
  <li>kilograms of material;</li>
  <li>hectares of land;</li>
  <li>hours of work.</li>
</ul>

<p>
These quantities cannot normally be negative.
</p>

<p>
You cannot produce -5 shirts or use -3 hectares of land.
</p>

<p>
Therefore we usually write:
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
<b>y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
These are called <b>non-negativity constraints</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
x represents the number of chairs produced.
</p>

<p>
Because a number of chairs cannot be negative:
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
y represents kilograms of maize produced.
</p>

<p>
The quantity cannot be negative:
</p>

<p>
<b>y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
x represents the number of shirts and y represents the number of
trousers.
</p>

<p>
Both quantities must be non-negative:
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
This means neither quantity can be less than zero.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
Non-negativity does not mean x and y must be positive.
</p>

<p>
Zero is allowed.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
x = 0
</p>

<p>
satisfies x ≥ 0.
</p>

<p>
This could mean that none of that product is produced.
</p>
', 1),
  (104, 104, '
<h2>Identifying Constraints in a Word Problem</h2>

<h3>The skill</h3>

<p>
Before writing equations or inequalities, we must identify the
conditions that restrict our choices.
</p>

<p>
These conditions are the <b>constraints</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A farmer has 40 hectares of land.
Maize uses x hectares and beans use y hectares.
</p>

<p>
The important restriction is:
</p>

<p>
The total land used cannot exceed 40 hectares.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 40</b>
</p>

<p>
The statement about 40 hectares is a constraint.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A factory has 100 hours available for two production processes.
Process A uses x hours and Process B uses y hours.
</p>

<p>
The restriction is:
</p>

<p>
The total time cannot exceed 100 hours.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 100</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A school must have at least 500 learners in two programmes.
Let x and y be the numbers of learners.
</p>

<p>
The restriction is:
</p>

<p>
The total number of learners must be at least 500.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≥ 500</b>
</p>

<h3>What to look for</h3>

<p>
Words such as:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>at most</li>
  <li>no more than</li>
  <li>cannot exceed</li>
  <li>at least</li>
  <li>not less than</li>
  <li>must have</li>
</ul>

<p>
often indicate a restriction.
</p>

<p>
Do not write an inequality just because you see numbers.
First ask:
</p>

<p>
<b>"What condition is limiting the choice?"</b>
</p>
