<h2>Shading the Region for ≥</h2>

<h3>The idea</h3>

<p>
For a ≥ inequality, we again use a test point.
We do not guess the side from the appearance of the graph.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≥ 10</b>
</p>

<p>
The boundary is:
</p>

<p>
x + y = 10
</p>

<p>
Test (0,0):
</p>

<p>
0 + 0 ≥ 10
</p>

<p>
0 ≥ 10
</p>

<p>
False.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the side containing (0,0) is not the solution region.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>2x + y ≥ 8</b>
</p>

<p>
Test (0,0):
</p>

<p>
2(0) + 0 ≥ 8
</p>

<p>
0 ≥ 8
</p>

<p>
False.
</p>

<p>
Therefore shade the opposite side from (0,0).
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>3x + 2y ≥ 12</b>
</p>

<p>
Test (5,2):
</p>

<p>
3(5) + 2(2) ≥ 12
</p>

<p>
15 + 4 ≥ 12
</p>

<p>
19 ≥ 12
</p>

<p>
True.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the region containing (5,2) satisfies the inequality.
</p>

<h3>Key method</h3>

<ol>
  <li>Draw the boundary.</li>
  <li>Choose a convenient test point.</li>
  <li>Substitute the coordinates.</li>
  <li>If the statement is true, that side is shaded.</li>
  <li>If false, shade the opposite side.</li>
</ol>
