<h2>Graphing an Inequality with Different Coefficients</h2>

<h3>Why are the coefficients important?</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>2x + 3y ≤ 12</b>
</p>

<p>
The x and y coefficients are different.
We cannot simply treat the boundary as x + y = 12.
</p>

<p>
Every coefficient must remain in the calculation.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Graph the boundary of:
</p>

<p>
<b>2x + 3y ≤ 12</b>
</p>

<p>
First find the boundary:
</p>

<p>
<b>2x + 3y = 12</b>
</p>

<p>
Find the x-intercept by setting y = 0:
</p>

<p>
2x = 12
</p>

<p>
x = 6
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
<b>(6,0)</b>
</p>

<p>
Find the y-intercept by setting x = 0:
</p>

<p>
3y = 12
</p>

<p>
y = 4
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
<b>(0,4)</b>
</p>

<p>
Plot (6,0) and (0,4), then draw the boundary.
</p>

<p>
Because the original inequality is ≤, the boundary is solid.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Graph:
</p>

<p>
<b>4x + 2y ≥ 16</b>
</p>

<p>
Boundary:
</p>

<p>
4x + 2y = 16
</p>

<p>
x-intercept:
</p>

<p>
4x = 16
</p>

<p>
x = 4
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
(4,0)
</p>

<p>
y-intercept:
</p>

<p>
2y = 16
</p>

<p>
y = 8
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
(0,8)
</p>

<p>
The boundary is solid because the inequality contains ≥.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Graph:
</p>

<p>
<b>3x + y ≤ 9</b>
</p>

<p>
Boundary:
</p>

<p>
3x + y = 9
</p>

<p>
x-intercept:
</p>

<p>
3x = 9
</p>

<p>
x = 3
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
(3,0)
</p>

<p>
y-intercept:
</p>

<p>
y = 9
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
(0,9)
</p>

<p>
Then test a point to determine the required side.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
The coefficients change the positions of the intercepts.
Always calculate them rather than guessing.
</p>
