<h2>Graphing Two Inequalities Together</h2>

<h3>The new idea</h3>

<p>
Sometimes a linear programming problem has more than one constraint.
Each constraint creates its own region.
</p>

<p>
The acceptable region must satisfy <b>both</b> inequalities at the
same time.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 8</b>
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 2</b>
</p>

<p>
The first inequality gives the boundary:
</p>

<p>
x + y = 8
</p>

<p>
The second gives:
</p>

<p>
x = 2
</p>

<p>
We graph both boundaries.
</p>

<p>
The first condition keeps points on the side satisfying
x + y ≤ 8.
</p>

<p>
The second keeps points where x ≥ 2.
</p>

<p>
The required region is where these two conditions overlap.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 10</b>
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + 2y ≤ 12</b>
</p>

<p>
Each inequality is graphed separately.
</p>

<p>
The final acceptable region is the part of the graph that satisfies
both conditions simultaneously.
</p>

<p>
A point that satisfies only one inequality is not enough.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 6</b>
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 1</b>
</p>

<p>
Consider the point (0,4).
</p>

<p>
First condition:
</p>

<p>
0 + 4 ≤ 6
</p>

<p>
True.
</p>

<p>
Second condition:
</p>

<p>
0 ≥ 1
</p>

<p>
False.
</p>

<p>
Therefore (0,4) is NOT in the common solution region.
</p>

<p>
Now consider (2,3):
</p>

<p>
2 + 3 ≤ 6
</p>

<p>
True.
</p>

<p>
2 ≥ 1
</p>

<p>
True.
</p>

<p>
Therefore (2,3) satisfies both constraints.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
When several inequalities are present:
</p>

<p>
<b>The solution is the overlap of all the required regions.</b>
</p>
