<h2>Checking Whether a Point Is Feasible</h2>

<h3>The method</h3>

<p>
To check whether a point is feasible, substitute its coordinates into
<b>every constraint</b>.
</p>

<p>
If every statement is true, the point is feasible.
</p>

<p>
If even one statement is false, the point is not feasible.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Constraints:
</p>

<p>
x + y ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
x ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
y ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
Test (3,5).
</p>

<p>
First:
</p>

<p>
3 + 5 ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
8 ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
True.
</p>

<p>
Second:
</p>

<p>
3 ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
True.
</p>

<p>
Third:
</p>

<p>
5 ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
True.
</p>

<p>
Therefore (3,5) is feasible.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Constraints:
</p>

<p>
x + y ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
x ≥ 2
</p>

<p>
y ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
Test (1,4).
</p>

<p>
1 + 4 ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
5 ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
True.
</p>

<p>
But:
</p>

<p>
1 ≥ 2
</p>

<p>
is false.
</p>

<p>
Therefore (1,4) is not feasible.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Constraints:
</p>

<p>
2x + y ≤ 12
</p>

<p>
x ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
y ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
Test (4,5).
</p>

<p>
2(4) + 5 ≤ 12
</p>

<p>
8 + 5 ≤ 12
</p>

<p>
13 ≤ 12
</p>

<p>
False.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the point is not feasible.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Do not stop after finding one true inequality.
Check <b>every</b> constraint.
