<h2>Reading a Feasible Region</h2>

<h3>What does a feasible region tell us?</h3>

<p>
Every point in the feasible region represents a combination of values
that obeys all the restrictions.
</p>

<p>
For example, if:
</p>

<p>
x = number of chairs
</p>

<p>
y = number of tables
</p>

<p>
then a point such as (4,6) represents:
</p>

<p>
4 chairs and 6 tables.
</p>

<p>
If that point lies inside the feasible region, the combination obeys
the constraints.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose x represents bags of maize and y represents bags of beans.
</p>

<p>
A point:
</p>

<p>
(5,3)
</p>

<p>
means:
</p>

<p>
5 bags of maize and 3 bags of beans.
</p>

<p>
If (5,3) is inside the feasible region, that combination is allowed.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
x = hours spent on Activity A
</p>

<p>
y = hours spent on Activity B
</p>

<p>
The point:
</p>

<p>
(4,2)
</p>

<p>
means 4 hours on A and 2 hours on B.
</p>

<p>
If it lies in the feasible region, the allocation is allowed by the
constraints.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
x = number of shirts
</p>

<p>
y = number of trousers.
</p>

<p>
The point:
</p>

<p>
(8,5)
</p>

<p>
represents producing 8 shirts and 5 trousers.
</p>

<p>
The graph lets us determine whether this combination satisfies the
constraints.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
A point on the graph is not just a coordinate.
In a linear programming problem, its coordinates represent actual
decision-variable values.
</p>
