<h2>Interpreting the Boundary and Feasible Region</h2>

<h3>What does the boundary mean?</h3>

<p>
A boundary line represents a limiting condition.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
x + y = 20
</p>

<p>
may represent using exactly 20 units of a resource.
</p>

<p>
The inequality:
</p>

<p>
x + y ≤ 20
</p>

<p>
means we may use up to 20 units, but not more.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
x = bags of maize
</p>

<p>
y = bags of beans
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
x + y ≤ 50
</p>

<p>
The boundary:
</p>

<p>
x + y = 50
</p>

<p>
represents using exactly 50 bags in total.
</p>

<p>
The feasible side represents combinations using 50 bags or fewer.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
x = hours on Machine A
</p>

<p>
y = hours on Machine B
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
2x + y ≤ 12
</p>

<p>
The boundary:
</p>

<p>
2x + y = 12
</p>

<p>
represents combinations that use exactly the maximum allowed amount
of the resource represented by the coefficients.
</p>

<p>
The feasible side represents combinations that remain within the
limit.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
x = number of desks
</p>

<p>
y = number of chairs
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
x + y ≤ 100.
</p>

<p>
A point such as (40,30) gives:
</p>

<p>
40 + 30 = 70.
</p>

<p>
Since:
</p>

<p>
70 ≤ 100,
</p>

<p>
the combination stays within the limit.
</p>

<p>
A point such as (70,50) gives:
</p>

<p>
70 + 50 = 120.
</p>

<p>
Since:
</p>

<p>
120 ≤ 100
</p>

<p>
is false, that combination is outside the feasible region.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
The graph is a mathematical picture of the restrictions.
The feasible region shows all combinations that are allowed.
</p>
