<h2>Meaning of an Objective Function</h2>

<p>
An <b>objective function</b> is the mathematical expression that tells us
what quantity we want to <b>maximize or minimize</b>.
</p>

<p>
In a linear programming problem, the variables represent the quantities
we are deciding. The objective function tells us what result we want
to make as large or as small as possible.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Profit</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Each table gives a profit of KSh 500.</li>
<li>Each chair gives a profit of KSh 200.</li>
</ul>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
<b>x</b> = number of tables<br>
<b>y</b> = number of chairs
</p>

<p>
Profit from x tables:
</p>

<p>
500x
</p>

<p>
Profit from y chairs:
</p>

<p>
200y
</p>

<p>
Therefore total profit is:
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 500x + 200y</b>
</p>

<p>
This is the <b>objective function</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Cost</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<ul>
<li>One bag of material A costs KSh 300.</li>
<li>One bag of material B costs KSh 450.</li>
</ul>

<p>
If x and y are the numbers of bags used:
</p>

<p>
Cost of A = 300x
</p>

<p>
Cost of B = 450y
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>C = 300x + 450y</b>
</p>

<p>
If the problem asks for the lowest possible cost, this is the
objective function to <b>minimize</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Identifying the Objective</h3>

<p>
A farmer produces x bags of maize and y bags of beans.
The profit is KSh 800 per bag of maize and KSh 600 per bag of beans.
</p>

<p>
Total profit:
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 800x + 600y</b>
</p>

<p>
If the farmer wants the greatest possible profit, the objective is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Maximize P = 800x + 600y</b>
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
<b>Constraints</b> tell us what is allowed.
</p>

<p>
The <b>objective function</b> tells us what we want to make as large
or as small as possible.
</p>
', 1),
  (128, 128, '
<h2>Identifying the Objective in a Word Problem</h2>

<p>
Before forming an objective function, first ask:
</p>

<p>
<b>"What quantity is the problem trying to make as large or as small as possible?"</b>
</p>

<p>
Words such as:
</p>

<ul>
<li>greatest profit</li>
<li>maximum revenue</li>
<li>largest number</li>
<li>minimum cost</li>
<li>lowest expenditure</li>
<li>least time</li>
</ul>

<p>
help identify the objective.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A shop sells two products. The owner wants to obtain the
<b>greatest profit</b>.
</p>

<p>
The objective is therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Profit</b>
</p>

<p>
The word <b>greatest</b> tells us that profit will be maximized.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A factory uses two materials and wants to produce its goods at the
<b>lowest possible cost</b>.
</p>

<p>
The objective is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Cost</b>
</p>

<p>
Because the cost must be as small as possible, it will be minimized.
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A farmer produces maize and beans and wants to obtain the
<b>maximum revenue</b>.
</p>

<p>
The objective is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Revenue</b>
</p>

<p>
The word <b>maximum</b> means that revenue must be made as large
as possible.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
The objective is not automatically "profit".
</p>

<p>
It could be:
</p>

<ul>
<li>profit</li>
<li>cost</li>
<li>revenue</li>
<li>production</li>
<li>time</li>
</ul>

<p>
The wording of the problem tells us which quantity is the objective.
</p>
', 1),
  (129, 129, '
<h2>Forming a Profit Objective Function</h2>

<p>
To form a profit objective function, find the profit earned from
each type of product and add the profits.
</p>

<p>
The basic structure is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Total profit = profit from first product + profit from second product</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A shop makes KSh 500 profit on each shirt and KSh 800 profit on
each pair of trousers.
</p>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
x = number of shirts<br>
y = number of pairs of trousers
</p>

<p>
Profit from shirts:
</p>

<p>
500 × x = 500x
</p>

<p>
Profit from trousers:
</p>

<p>
800 × y = 800y
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 500x + 800y</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A bakery earns KSh 30 profit per loaf and KSh 50 profit per cake.
Let x be the number of loaves and y the number of cakes.
</p>

<p>
Loaf profit:
</p>

<p>
30x
</p>

<p>
Cake profit:
</p>

<p>
50y
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 30x + 50y</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A farmer earns KSh 1,200 profit from each bag of maize and
KSh 900 profit from each bag of beans.
</p>

<p>
Let x = bags of maize and y = bags of beans.
</p>

<p>
Maize profit = 1200x
</p>

<p>
Bean profit = 900y
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 1200x + 900y</b>
</p>

<p>
If the aim is greatest profit:
</p>

<p>
<b>Maximize P = 1200x + 900y</b>
</p>
', 1),
  (130, 130, '
<h2>Forming a Cost Objective Function</h2>

<p>
A cost objective function represents the total amount spent on the
different quantities being considered.
</p>

<p>
The same idea used for profit applies:
</p>

<p>
<b>Total cost = cost of first item + cost of second item</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A factory uses:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Material A costing KSh 300 per unit</li>
<li>Material B costing KSh 500 per unit</li>
</ul>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
x = units of A<br>
y = units of B
</p>

<p>
Cost of A = 300x
</p>

<p>
Cost of B = 500y
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>C = 300x + 500y</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A farmer buys fertilizer A at KSh 2,000 per bag and fertilizer B
at KSh 1,500 per bag.
</p>

<p>
Let x and y be the numbers of bags.
</p>

<p>
Cost:
</p>

<p>
C = 2000x + 1500y
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A school buys notebooks at KSh 40 each and textbooks at KSh 250 each.
</p>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
x = notebooks<br>
y = textbooks
</p>

<p>
Notebook cost = 40x
</p>

<p>
Textbook cost = 250y
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>C = 40x + 250y</b>
</p>

<p>
If the problem asks for the lowest total cost, the objective is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Minimize C</b>
</p>
