<h2>Evaluating an Objective Function at a Point</h2>

<p>
Once an objective function has been formed, we can calculate its value
for a particular point.
</p>

<p>
This means replacing x and y with the coordinates of the point.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
Given:
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 500x + 300y</b>
</p>

<p>
Find P at the point (4, 2).
</p>

<p>
The point (4, 2) means:
</p>

<p>
x = 4<br>
y = 2
</p>

<p>
Substitute:
</p>

<p>
P = 500(4) + 300(2)
</p>

<p>
P = 2000 + 600
</p>

<p>
<b>P = KSh 2,600</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
Given:
</p>

<p>
<b>C = 200x + 500y</b>
</p>

<p>
Find C at (3, 4).
</p>

<p>
x = 3 and y = 4.
</p>

<p>
C = 200(3) + 500(4)
</p>

<p>
C = 600 + 2000
</p>

<p>
<b>C = KSh 2,600</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
Given:
</p>

<p>
<b>R = 1000x + 1500y</b>
</p>

<p>
Find R at (5, 2).
</p>

<p>
R = 1000(5) + 1500(2)
</p>

<p>
R = 5000 + 3000
</p>

<p>
<b>R = KSh 8,000</b>
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
A point such as (4,2) gives the values of the variables:
</p>

<p>
<b>x = 4 and y = 2</b>
</p>

<p>
Those values are then substituted into the objective function.
</p>
', 1),
  (134, 134, '
<h2>Comparing Objective Values at Two Points</h2>

<p>
Two feasible points can give different values of the same objective
function.
</p>

<p>
To compare them, calculate the objective value at each point.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 500x + 300y</b>
</p>

<p>
Compare A(4,2) and B(2,5).
</p>

<p>
For A:
</p>

<p>
P = 500(4) + 300(2)
</p>

<p>
P = 2000 + 600
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 2600</b>
</p>

<p>
For B:
</p>

<p>
P = 500(2) + 300(5)
</p>

<p>
P = 1000 + 1500
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 2500</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore A gives the greater profit.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
C = 200x + 400y.
Compare (3,2) and (1,5).
</p>

<p>
At (3,2):
</p>

<p>
C = 200(3) + 400(2)
</p>

<p>
C = 600 + 800 = 1400
</p>

<p>
At (1,5):
</p>

<p>
C = 200(1) + 400(5)
</p>

<p>
C = 200 + 2000 = 2200
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>2200 > 1400</b>
</p>

<p>
So (1,5) gives the greater cost.
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
R = 100x + 200y.
Compare (5,1) and (2,4).
</p>

<p>
At (5,1):
</p>

<p>
R = 100(5) + 200(1) = 500 + 200 = 700
</p>

<p>
At (2,4):
</p>

<p>
R = 100(2) + 200(4) = 200 + 800 = 1000
</p>

<p>
Therefore (2,4) gives the greater revenue.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
<b>Never decide which point is better just by looking at x or y.
Calculate the objective value.</b>
</p>
', 1),
  (135, 135, '
<h2>Meaning of Maximisation</h2>

<p>
<b>Maximisation</b> means finding the greatest possible value of
the objective function.
</p>

<p>
For example, if:
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 500x + 300y</b>
</p>

<p>
represents profit, then maximising P means finding the allowed
combination of x and y that gives the <b>largest possible profit</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose the feasible points are:
</p>

<p>
(2,3), (4,2), (1,5)
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
P = 500x + 300y
</p>

<p>
At (2,3):
</p>

<p>
P = 500(2) + 300(3) = 1000 + 900 = 1900
</p>

<p>
At (4,2):
</p>

<p>
P = 500(4) + 300(2) = 2000 + 600 = 2600
</p>

<p>
At (1,5):
</p>

<p>
P = 500(1) + 300(5) = 500 + 1500 = 2000
</p>

<p>
The greatest value is:
</p>

<p>
<b>2600</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore the best point among these three is:
</p>

<p>
<b>(4,2)</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
If revenue is:
</p>

<p>
R = 100x + 250y
</p>

<p>
and the problem asks for maximum revenue, we want the feasible
point that gives the largest value of R.
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
If:
</p>

<p>
P = 700x + 400y
</p>

<p>
then a larger value of P means a larger profit.
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
<b>Maximise P</b>
</p>

<p>
means:
</p>

<p>
<b>Find the greatest possible value of P.</b>
</p>
', 1),
  (136, 136, '
<h2>Meaning of Minimisation</h2>

<p>
<b>Minimisation</b> means finding the smallest possible value of
the objective function.
</p>

<p>
For example, suppose:
</p>

<p>
<b>C = 400x + 600y</b>
</p>

<p>
represents total cost.
</p>

<p>
If the objective is to minimise C, we want the allowed combination
of x and y that gives the <b>lowest possible cost</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose three feasible points give these costs:
</p>

<ul>
<li>(2,3) → KSh 2,600</li>
<li>(4,1) → KSh 2,200</li>
<li>(1,4) → KSh 2,800</li>
</ul>

<p>
The smallest value is:
</p>

<p>
<b>KSh 2,200</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore the second point is preferred when minimising cost.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
C = 300x + 500y
</p>

<p>
At A:
</p>

<p>
C = 1800
</p>

<p>
At B:
</p>

<p>
C = 2400
</p>

<p>
Because the objective is to minimise cost:
</p>

<p>
<b>1800 is preferred to 2400.</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose a company wants to minimise transport cost.
Three allowed plans give:
</p>

<p>
Plan A = KSh 15,000
</p>

<p>
Plan B = KSh 12,500
</p>

<p>
Plan C = KSh 14,000
</p>

<p>
The smallest cost is:
</p>

<p>
<b>KSh 12,500</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore Plan B gives the minimum cost among the three plans.
</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>

<p>
<b>Maximise → choose the greatest value.</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Minimise → choose the smallest value.</b>
</p>
