<h2>Evaluating the Objective at Every Corner Point</h2>

<p>
Once the corner points have been found, substitute each one into
the objective function.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 400x + 300y</b>
</p>

<p>
and the feasible region has corners:
</p>

<p>
A(0,0), B(5,0), C(3,4), D(0,4)
</p>

<h3>Step 1: Evaluate A(0,0)</h3>

<p>
x = 0, y = 0
</p>

<p>
P = 400(0) + 300(0)
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 0</b>
</p>

<h3>Step 2: Evaluate B(5,0)</h3>

<p>
P = 400(5) + 300(0)
</p>

<p>
P = 2000
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 2,000</b>
</p>

<h3>Step 3: Evaluate C(3,4)</h3>

<p>
P = 400(3) + 300(4)
</p>

<p>
P = 1200 + 1200
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 2,400</b>
</p>

<h3>Step 4: Evaluate D(0,4)</h3>

<p>
P = 400(0) + 300(4)
</p>

<p>
P = 1200
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 1,200</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore the objective values are:
</p>

<ul>
<li>A → 0</li>
<li>B → 2,000</li>
<li>C → 2,400</li>
<li>D → 1,200</li>
</ul>

<p>
We have not yet chosen the answer.
</p>

<p>
First we calculate all the objective values.
</p>

<p>
The next step is to compare them according to whether we are
maximizing or minimizing.
</p>
