<h2>Finding the Maximum from Corner Values</h2>

<p>
When the objective is to be <b>maximized</b>, choose the greatest
objective value among the feasible corner points.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose the corner values are:
</p>

<ul>
<li>A → 1,200</li>
<li>B → 1,800</li>
<li>C → 2,500</li>
<li>D → 2,000</li>
</ul>

<p>
The greatest value is:
</p>

<p>
<b>2,500</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore C gives the maximum objective value.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
P = 500x + 300y
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<ul>
<li>(0,0) → P = 0</li>
<li>(4,0) → P = 2,000</li>
<li>(3,4) → P = 2,700</li>
<li>(0,5) → P = 1,500</li>
</ul>

<p>
Compare:
</p>

<p>
0, 2000, 2700, 1500
</p>

<p>
The greatest value is 2700.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Maximum P = 2,700</b>
</p>

<p>
at:
</p>

<p>
<b>(3,4)</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose three feasible corner points give profits:
</p>

<ul>
<li>(2,1) → KSh 1,500</li>
<li>(5,2) → KSh 3,400</li>
<li>(1,5) → KSh 2,600</li>
</ul>

<p>
The greatest profit is KSh 3,400.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the optimal point is:
</p>

<p>
<b>(5,2)</b>
</p>

<h3>Rule</h3>

<p>
<b>Maximization → choose the greatest objective value.</b>
