<h2>Finding the Optimal Point and Optimal Value</h2>

<p>
There are two different things we must report:
</p>

<p>
<b>1. The optimal point</b> — the values of x and y that give the
best result.
</p>

<p>
<b>2. The optimal value</b> — the maximum or minimum value of the
objective function.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 500x + 300y</b>
</p>

<p>
The corner points and objective values are:
</p>

<ul>
<li>(0,0) → P = 0</li>
<li>(4,0) → P = 2,000</li>
<li>(3,4) → P = 2,700</li>
<li>(0,5) → P = 1,500</li>
</ul>

<p>
The largest value is 2,700.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Optimal point = (3,4)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Maximum profit = KSh 2,700</b>
</p>

<h3>Why both answers matter</h3>

<p>
The point:
</p>

<p>
<b>(3,4)</b>
</p>

<p>
tells us the quantities:
</p>

<p>
x = 3
</p>

<p>
y = 4
</p>

<p>
The value:
</p>

<p>
<b>KSh 2,700</b>
</p>

<p>
tells us the resulting maximum profit.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
Suppose a cost function gives:
</p>

<ul>
<li>(2,1) → KSh 1,400</li>
<li>(4,2) → KSh 2,000</li>
<li>(1,3) → KSh 1,200</li>
</ul>

<p>
For minimum cost:
</p>

<p>
<b>Optimal point = (1,3)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Minimum cost = KSh 1,200</b>
</p>

<h3>Exam habit</h3>

<p>
Do not write only:
</p>

<p>
<b>"Maximum = 2,700"</b>
</p>

<p>
Also identify the point:
</p>

<p>
<b>"at (3,4)"</b>
</p>
