<h2>Defining Decision Variables</h2>

<p>
Before writing inequalities or an objective function, we must decide
what the variables represent.
</p>

<p>
A <b>decision variable</b> represents a quantity that we are allowed
to choose.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A bakery makes bread and cakes.
</p>

<p>
We need to decide how many bread loaves and cakes to make.
</p>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
<b>x = number of loaves of bread</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>y = number of cakes</b>
</p>

<p>
Now x and y have clear meanings.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A farmer produces maize and beans.
</p>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
<b>x = number of bags of maize</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>y = number of bags of beans</b>
</p>

<p>
These are decision variables because the farmer decides how many
bags of each crop to produce.
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A company makes tables and chairs.
</p>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
<b>x = number of tables produced</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>y = number of chairs produced</b>
</p>

<p>
Every later equation must use these meanings consistently.
</p>

<h3>Why this matters</h3>

<p>
If we define x incorrectly, every inequality and the objective
function built from x will also be wrong.
</p>

<p>
So the first question in a word problem is:
</p>

<p>
<b>"What quantities are we deciding?"</b>
</p>
', 1),
  (147, 147, '
<h2>Translating "At Most" into an Inequality</h2>

<p>
The phrase <b>at most</b> means <b>no more than</b>.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>at most 20 → ≤ 20</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A farmer can use <b>at most 50 kg</b> of fertilizer.
</p>

<p>
If x is the amount of fertilizer used:
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≤ 50</b>
</p>

<p>
Why?
</p>

<p>
The farmer may use 50 kg, or less, but not more than 50 kg.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A factory has <b>at most 100 machine hours</b>.
</p>

<p>
If x and y are the hours used by two products:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 100</b>
</p>

<p>
The total can be 100 or less.
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A shop can store <b>at most 80 items</b>.
</p>

<p>
If x shirts and y trousers are stored:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 80</b>
</p>

<p>
The total cannot exceed 80.
</p>

<h3>Translation rule</h3>

<p>
<b>"At most" → ≤</b>
</p>
', 1),
  (148, 148, '
<h2>Translating "At Least" into an Inequality</h2>

<p>
The phrase <b>at least</b> means <b>no less than</b>.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>at least 20 → ≥ 20</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A school must have <b>at least 200 students</b> in a programme.
</p>

<p>
If x is the number of students:
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 200</b>
</p>

<p>
The school can have 200 students or more.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A farmer must produce <b>at least 30 bags</b> of maize and beans
combined.
</p>

<p>
Let x = maize bags and y = bean bags.
</p>

<p>
The total production is:
</p>

<p>
x + y
</p>

<p>
At least 30 means:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≥ 30</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A company must make at least 10 tables.
</p>

<p>
If x represents tables:
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 10</b>
</p>

<p>
The company cannot produce fewer than 10 tables.
</p>

<h3>Translation rule</h3>

<p>
<b>"At least" → ≥</b>
</p>
