<h2>Translating a Resource Limit into a Constraint</h2>

<p>
Many linear programming problems involve a limited resource such as:
</p>

<ul>
<li>time</li>
<li>money</li>
<li>raw materials</li>
<li>land</li>
<li>storage space</li>
</ul>

<p>
To form the constraint, calculate how much of the resource each
activity uses and compare the total with the available amount.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Machine Time</h3>

<p>
A table uses 3 machine hours.
A chair uses 2 machine hours.
The factory has at most 60 machine hours.
</p>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
x = tables<br>
y = chairs
</p>

<p>
Time used by tables:
</p>

<p>
3x
</p>

<p>
Time used by chairs:
</p>

<p>
2y
</p>

<p>
Total time:
</p>

<p>
3x + 2y
</p>

<p>
Available time = 60.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>3x + 2y ≤ 60</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Raw Material</h3>

<p>
Product A uses 4 kg of material.
Product B uses 5 kg.
Only 100 kg is available.
</p>

<p>
If x and y are the quantities:
</p>

<p>
Material used = 4x + 5y
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>4x + 5y ≤ 100</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Money</h3>

<p>
Product A costs KSh 200 to produce.
Product B costs KSh 300.
The available budget is KSh 6,000.
</p>

<p>
Total production cost:
</p>

<p>
200x + 300y
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>200x + 300y ≤ 6000</b>
</p>

<h3>Pattern</h3>

<p>
<b>Resource used ≤ Resource available</b>
</p>
