<h2>Translating a Production Requirement into a Constraint</h2>

<p>
Not every constraint comes from a resource limit.
</p>

<p>
Sometimes the problem tells us that a certain minimum or maximum
amount must be produced.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A company must produce <b>at least 20 tables</b>.
</p>

<p>
Let x = number of tables.
</p>

<p>
At least 20 means:
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 20</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A bakery must produce <b>at least 50 items altogether</b>.
It makes bread and cakes.
</p>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
x = bread<br>
y = cakes
</p>

<p>
Total production = x + y
</p>

<p>
At least 50:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≥ 50</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A factory can make <b>at most 100 products altogether</b>.
</p>

<p>
If x and y are the quantities of two products:
</p>

<p>
Total production = x + y
</p>

<p>
At most 100:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 100</b>
</p>

<h3>Notice the difference</h3>

<p>
<b>At least 50:</b>
</p>

<p>
x + y ≥ 50
</p>

<p>
<b>At most 50:</b>
</p>

<p>
x + y ≤ 50
</p>

<p>
One word changes the direction of the inequality.
', 1),
  (151, 151, '
<h2>Translating Non-Negativity Conditions</h2>

<p>
In a production problem, a quantity normally cannot be negative.
</p>

<p>
You cannot produce -5 tables.
</p>

<p>
Therefore decision variables usually satisfy:
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
<b>y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
If x represents the number of shirts:
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
This says the number of shirts cannot be negative.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
If x represents tables and y represents chairs:
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
Both quantities must be non-negative.
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose a problem has:
</p>

<p>
2x + 3y ≤ 60
</p>

<p>
Even though the inequality limits the resource, we also need:
</p>

<p>
x ≥ 0, y ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
because x and y represent quantities being produced.
</p>

<h3>Why this matters</h3>

<p>
Without non-negativity restrictions, a graph could include negative
values that have no meaning for the production problem.
</p>
