<h2>Translating Cost Wording into an Objective Function</h2>

<p>
Some problems ask us to spend as little as possible.
In such cases, the objective is <b>cost</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A company uses material A at KSh 300 per unit and material B at
KSh 500 per unit.
</p>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
x = units of A<br>
y = units of B
</p>

<p>
Cost of A = 300x
</p>

<p>
Cost of B = 500y
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>C = 300x + 500y</b>
</p>

<p>
If the aim is the lowest cost:
</p>

<p>
<b>Minimize C = 300x + 500y</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A school buys notebooks at KSh 50 each and textbooks at KSh 250 each.
</p>

<p>
Let x = notebooks and y = textbooks.
</p>

<p>
Total cost:
</p>

<p>
<b>C = 50x + 250y</b>
</p>

<p>
If the aim is minimum expenditure:
</p>

<p>
<b>Minimize C</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A farmer buys fertilizer A at KSh 2,000 per bag and fertilizer B
at KSh 1,500 per bag.
</p>

<p>
Let x and y represent the bags purchased.
</p>

<p>
<b>C = 2000x + 1500y</b>
</p>

<p>
The objective is to minimize C if the farmer wants the lowest cost.
