<h2>Building a Complete Linear Programming Model</h2>

<p>
A complete model contains three main parts:
</p>

<ol>
<li><b>Decision variables</b></li>
<li><b>Constraints</b></li>
<li><b>Objective function</b></li>
</ol>

<p>
We may also include:
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0 and y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>
A factory makes tables and chairs.
</p>

<p>
Each table uses 3 hours of labour.
Each chair uses 2 hours.
The factory has at most 60 labour hours.
</p>

<p>
The factory can make at most 20 items altogether.
</p>

<p>
Profit is KSh 500 per table and KSh 300 per chair.
The factory wants maximum profit.
</p>

<h3>Step 1: Define variables</h3>

<p>
x = number of tables
</p>

<p>
y = number of chairs
</p>

<h3>Step 2: Labour constraint</h3>

<p>
Tables use 3x hours.
</p>

<p>
Chairs use 2y hours.
</p>

<p>
Total labour = 3x + 2y.
</p>

<p>
At most 60 hours:
</p>

<p>
<b>3x + 2y ≤ 60</b>
</p>

<h3>Step 3: Production constraint</h3>

<p>
Total items = x + y.
</p>

<p>
At most 20:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 20</b>
</p>

<h3>Step 4: Non-negativity</h3>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<h3>Step 5: Objective</h3>

<p>
Profit from tables = 500x.
</p>

<p>
Profit from chairs = 300y.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Maximize P = 500x + 300y</b>
</p>

<h3>Complete model</h3>

<p>
<b>Maximize P = 500x + 300y</b>
</p>

<p>
subject to:
</p>

<p>
<b>3x + 2y ≤ 60</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 20</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0, y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
This is the mathematical model of the written problem.
', 1),
  (155, 155, '
<h2>Translating a Full Word Problem into Constraints</h2>

<p>
A single word problem can contain several different restrictions.
Each restriction must become its own constraint.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>
A farmer grows maize and beans.
</p>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
x = bags of maize
</p>

<p>
y = bags of beans
</p>

<p>
The farmer has at most 40 acres.
Each bag of maize requires 2 acres and each bag of beans requires
1 acre.
</p>

<p>
The farmer must produce at least 15 bags altogether.
</p>

<h3>Constraint 1: Land</h3>

<p>
Maize requires:
</p>

<p>
2x acres
</p>

<p>
Beans require:
</p>

<p>
y acres
</p>

<p>
Total land used:
</p>

<p>
2x + y
</p>

<p>
At most 40 acres:
</p>

<p>
<b>2x + y ≤ 40</b>
</p>

<h3>Constraint 2: Minimum production</h3>

<p>
Total bags:
</p>

<p>
x + y
</p>

<p>
At least 15:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≥ 15</b>
</p>

<h3>Constraint 3: Non-negativity</h3>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<h3>Complete constraints</h3>

<p>
<b>2x + y ≤ 40</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≥ 15</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0, y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
Notice that each inequality came from a different sentence in the
word problem.
', 1),
  (156, 156, '
<h2>Checking Whether a Proposed Production Plan Is Feasible</h2>

<p>
After creating a model, we can test whether a proposed production
plan satisfies all the constraints.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
2x + y ≤ 40
</p>

<p>
x + y ≥ 15
</p>

<p>
x ≥ 0, y ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
Test the proposed plan:
</p>

<p>
<b>(10,10)</b>
</p>

<h3>Check Constraint 1</h3>

<p>
2(10) + 10 ≤ 40
</p>

<p>
20 + 10 ≤ 40
</p>

<p>
30 ≤ 40
</p>

<p>
True.
</p>

<h3>Check Constraint 2</h3>

<p>
10 + 10 ≥ 15
</p>

<p>
20 ≥ 15
</p>

<p>
True.
</p>

<h3>Check non-negativity</h3>

<p>
10 ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
10 ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
Both are true.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>(10,10) is feasible.</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
Test (15,10).
</p>

<p>
2(15) + 10 = 40
</p>

<p>
The first constraint is satisfied.
</p>

<p>
15 + 10 = 25
</p>

<p>
The second is also satisfied.
</p>

<p>
Therefore (15,10) is feasible.
</p>

<h3>Important rule</h3>

<p>
A proposed plan is feasible only if it satisfies
<b>every constraint</b>.
</p>
