  (217, 217, '
<h2>Finding the Mean of Grouped Data</h2>

<p>
When data is grouped into class intervals, we do not know the exact
value of every observation.
</p>

<p>
For example, if we have the class:
</p>

<p><b>10–19</b></p>

<p>
we know that observations in this class lie between 10 and 19, but we
do not know the individual values.
</p>

<p>
To estimate the mean, we represent each class using its
<b>midpoint</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Midpoint</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>14.5</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>24.5</td><td>3</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
For the first class:
</p>

<p>
Midpoint = (10 + 19) ÷ 2 = <b>14.5</b>
</p>

<p>
For the second class:
</p>

<p>
Midpoint = (20 + 29) ÷ 2 = <b>24.5</b>
</p>

<h3>Step 1: Calculate fx</h3>

<p>
For 10–19:
</p>

<p>
14.5 × 2 = <b>29</b>
</p>

<p>
For 20–29:
</p>

<p>
24.5 × 3 = <b>73.5</b>
</p>

<h3>Step 2: Find Σfx</h3>

<p>
29 + 73.5 = <b>102.5</b>
</p>

<h3>Step 3: Find Σf</h3>

<p>
2 + 3 = <b>5</b>
</p>

<h3>Step 4: Find the mean</h3>

<p>
Mean = 102.5 ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
Mean = <b>20.5</b>
</p>

<p>
Because the original individual values are unknown, this is an
<b>estimated mean</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Midpoint</th><th>f</th><th>fx</th></tr>
<tr><td>0–9</td><td>4.5</td><td>2</td><td>9</td></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>14.5</td><td>4</td><td>58</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>24.5</td><td>2</td><td>49</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Σfx = 9 + 58 + 49 = <b>116</b>
</p>

<p>
Σf = 2 + 4 + 2 = <b>8</b>
</p>

<p>
Mean = 116 ÷ 8 = <b>14.5</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Midpoint</th><th>f</th><th>fx</th></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>14.5</td><td>1</td><td>14.5</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>24.5</td><td>2</td><td>49</td></tr>
<tr><td>30–39</td><td>34.5</td><td>3</td><td>103.5</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Σfx = 14.5 + 49 + 103.5 = <b>167</b>
</p>

<p>
Σf = 1 + 2 + 3 = <b>6</b>
</p>

<p>
Mean = 167 ÷ 6
</p>

<p>
Mean = <b>27.83</b> approximately.
</p>

<p>
Remember: grouped-data mean uses the midpoint as a representative
value for each class.
</p>
', 1),
  (218, 218, '
<h2>Why Do We Use Midpoints?</h2>

<p>
When data is grouped, we lose the exact individual values.
</p>

<p>
Suppose a class interval is:
</p>

<p><b>20–29</b></p>

<p>
An observation in this class could be 20, 21, 22, 23, and so on up
to 29.
</p>

<p>
But the grouped table does not tell us which exact values occurred.
</p>

<p>
We therefore use one value to represent the whole class.
That representative value is the <b>midpoint</b>.
</p>

<h3>Finding the midpoint</h3>

<p>
<b>Midpoint = (Lower limit + Upper limit) ÷ 2</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Find the midpoint of 10–19.
</p>

<p>
Midpoint = (10 + 19) ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= 29 ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= <b>14.5</b>
</p>

<p>
So 14.5 is used as the representative value of the class 10–19.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Find the midpoint of 30–39.
</p>

<p>
Midpoint = (30 + 39) ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= 69 ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= <b>34.5</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A grouped table contains:
</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>0–9</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>4</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The exact values inside each class are unknown.
</p>

<p>
For 0–9:
</p>

<p>
Midpoint = (0 + 9) ÷ 2 = <b>4.5</b>
</p>

<p>
For 10–19:
</p>

<p>
Midpoint = (10 + 19) ÷ 2 = <b>14.5</b>
</p>

<p>
We can then use those midpoints in the grouped mean calculation.
</p>

<h3>Important warning</h3>

<p>
The midpoint does <b>not</b> mean every observation in the class
actually equals the midpoint.
</p>

<p>
For example, the class 10–19 could contain 10, 12, 17 and 19.
They are not all 14.5.
</p>

<p>
We use 14.5 because the exact values are unavailable.
That is why the grouped mean is an <b>estimate</b>.
</p>
', 1),
  (219, 219, '
<h2>Finding a Missing Value from a Known Mean</h2>

<p>
Sometimes the mean is given, but one data value is missing.
We can work backwards to find the missing value.
</p>

<h3>The key idea</h3>

<p>
We know:
</p>

<p>
<b>Mean = Total ÷ Number of values</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Total = Mean × Number of values</b>
</p>

<p>
Once we know the required total, subtract the known values.
The remainder is the missing value.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
The mean of 4, 6 and x is 8.
Find x.
</p>

<p>
There are 3 values.
</p>

<p>
Required total = Mean × Number of values
</p>

<p>
= 8 × 3
</p>

<p>
= <b>24</b>
</p>

<p>
The known values total:
</p>

<p>
4 + 6 = <b>10</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
x = 24 − 10
</p>

<p>
x = <b>14</b>
</p>

<p>
Check:
</p>

<p>
(4 + 6 + 14) ÷ 3 = 24 ÷ 3 = 8
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
The mean of 5 numbers is 12.
Four of the numbers are 8, 10, 14 and 15.
Find the fifth number.
</p>

<p>
Required total:
</p>

<p>
12 × 5 = <b>60</b>
</p>

<p>
Known total:
</p>

<p>
8 + 10 + 14 + 15 = <b>47</b>
</p>

<p>
Missing value:
</p>

<p>
60 − 47 = <b>13</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
The mean of six values is 9.
Five values are:
</p>

<p><b>4, 7, 8, 10, 12</b></p>

<p>
Required total:
</p>

<p>
9 × 6 = <b>54</b>
</p>

<p>
Known total:
</p>

<p>
4 + 7 + 8 + 10 + 12 = <b>41</b>
</p>

<p>
Missing value:
</p>

<p>
54 − 41 = <b>13</b>
</p>

<h3>General method</h3>

<ol>
<li>Multiply the mean by the number of values.</li>
<li>Add the known values.</li>
<li>Subtract the known total from the required total.</li>
<li>Check by calculating the mean again.</li>
</ol>
', 1),
  (220, 220, '
<h2>Finding a Missing Frequency from a Known Mean</h2>

<p>
A frequency tells us how many times a value occurs.
Sometimes one frequency is unknown, but the mean is given.
</p>

<p>
We can find the missing frequency by using:
</p>

<p>
<b>Mean = Σfx ÷ Σf</b>
</p>

<p>
The important point is that the missing frequency appears in
<b>both the numerator and denominator</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
The table below has a mean of 4.
Find f.
</p>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>f</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
For x = 2:
</p>

<p>
fx = 2 × 3 = 6
</p>

<p>
For x = 4:
</p>

<p>
fx = 4 × f = 4f
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
Σfx = 6 + 4f
</p>

<p>
And:
</p>

<p>
Σf = 3 + f
</p>

<p>
The mean is 4:
</p>

<p>
4 = (6 + 4f) ÷ (3 + f)
</p>

<p>
Multiply both sides by (3 + f):
</p>

<p>
4(3 + f) = 6 + 4f
</p>

<p>
12 + 4f = 6 + 4f
</p>

<p>
This equation has no unique solution because the two sides reduce
to the same relationship only when the known value already equals
the mean.
</p>

<p>
Therefore this particular table cannot determine one unique
missing frequency.
</p>

<h3>Why this matters</h3>

<p>
A good statistics question must contain enough information to
determine the unknown.
If the unknown frequency belongs to a value equal to the mean,
changing its frequency does not change the mean.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
The table has mean 5.
Find f.
</p>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>f</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>2</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Calculate known fx values:
</p>

<p>
2 × 2 = 4
</p>

<p>
8 × 2 = 16
</p>

<p>
The unknown contribution is:
</p>

<p>
5f
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
Σfx = 4 + 5f + 16
</p>

<p>
= 20 + 5f
</p>

<p>
Σf = 2 + f + 2
</p>

<p>
= 4 + f
</p>

<p>
Using the mean:
</p>

<p>
5 = (20 + 5f) ÷ (4 + f)
</p>

<p>
5(4 + f) = 20 + 5f
</p>

<p>
20 + 5f = 20 + 5f
</p>

<p>
Again, the equation is true for every valid f.
The missing frequency cannot be uniquely determined.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3 — A Determinable Case</h3>

<p>
The table has mean 4.
Find f.
</p>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>f</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
For x = 2:
</p>

<p>
fx = 2 × 1 = 2
</p>

<p>
For x = 6:
</p>

<p>
fx = 6f
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
Σfx = 2 + 6f
</p>

<p>
Σf = 1 + f
</p>

<p>
Mean = 4:
</p>

<p>
4 = (2 + 6f) ÷ (1 + f)
</p>

<p>
Multiply:
</p>

<p>
4(1 + f) = 2 + 6f
</p>

<p>
4 + 4f = 2 + 6f
</p>

<p>
4 − 2 = 6f − 4f
</p>

<p>
2 = 2f
</p>

<p>
f = <b>1</b>
</p>

<p>
Check:
</p>

<p>
The table becomes:
</p>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th><th>fx</th></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>1</td><td>6</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Mean = (2 + 6) ÷ (1 + 1)
</p>

<p>
= 8 ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= <b>4</b>
</p>

<h3>Important lesson</h3>

<p>
Before solving for a missing frequency, check whether the problem
actually gives enough information for a unique answer.
</p>
', 1),
  (221, 221, '
<h2>Meaning of the Median</h2>

<p>
The <b>median</b> is the middle value of a set of data after the
values have been arranged in order.
</p>

<p>
The most important rule is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Always arrange the data in ascending or descending order first.</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Find the median of:
</p>

<p><b>3, 8, 5, 2, 7</b></p>

<p>
The values are not in order.
</p>

<p>
Arrange them from smallest to largest:
</p>

<p>
2, 3, 5, 7, 8
</p>

<p>
There are 5 values.
</p>

<p>
The middle value is 5.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Median = 5</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Find the median of:
</p>

<p><b>10, 4, 7, 2, 6</b></p>

<p>
Arrange:
</p>

<p>
2, 4, 6, 7, 10
</p>

<p>
The middle value is 6.
</p>

<p><b>Median = 6</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Find the median of:
</p>

<p><b>12, 5, 9, 3, 8, 15, 6</b></p>

<p>
Arrange:
</p>

<p>
3, 5, 6, 8, 9, 12, 15
</p>

<p>
There are 7 values.
</p>

<p>
The fourth value is in the middle:
</p>

<p>
3, 5, 6, <b>8</b>, 9, 12, 15
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Median = 8</b></p>

<h3>Why must we arrange the data?</h3>

<p>
The median depends on position.
If the data is not ordered, we cannot correctly identify which
value is in the middle.
</p>

<p>
So remember:
</p>

<p>
<b>Order → Count → Find the middle.</b>
</p>
', 1),
  (222, 222, '
<h2>Median of an Odd-Sized Dataset</h2>

<p>
When the number of observations is <b>odd</b>, there is exactly one
middle observation.
</p>

<p>
For an odd number of observations, the position of the median is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Median position = (n + 1) ÷ 2</b>
</p>

<p>
where <b>n</b> is the number of observations.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Find the median of:
</p>

<p><b>4, 9, 2, 7, 5</b></p>

<p>
There are:
</p>

<p>
n = 5
</p>

<p>
Median position:
</p>

<p>
(5 + 1) ÷ 2 = 6 ÷ 2 = <b>3rd position</b>
</p>

<p>
Order the data:
</p>

<p>
2, 4, <b>5</b>, 7, 9
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Median = 5</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
There are 9 observations.
The median position is:
</p>

<p>
(9 + 1) ÷ 2 = 10 ÷ 2 = <b>5th position</b>
</p>

<p>
So after arranging the data, the fifth value is the median.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Find the median of:
</p>

<p><b>15, 3, 9, 7, 11, 5, 13</b></p>

<p>
There are 7 observations.
</p>

<p>
Median position:
</p>

<p>
(7 + 1) ÷ 2 = <b>4th position</b>
</p>

<p>
Arrange:
</p>

<p>
3, 5, 7, <b>9</b>, 11, 13, 15
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Median = 9</b></p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
The formula gives the <b>position</b>, not the median itself.
</p>

<p>
You still have to arrange the data and take the value at that
position.
</p>
', 1),
  (223, 223, '
<h2>Median of an Even-Sized Dataset</h2>

<p>
When the number of observations is <b>even</b>, there is no single
middle observation.
</p>

<p>
There are two middle values.
</p>

<p>
The median is the average of those two middle values.
</p>

<p>
<b>Median = (First middle value + Second middle value) ÷ 2</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Find the median of:
</p>

<p><b>4, 8, 2, 6</b></p>

<p>
First arrange:
</p>

<p>
2, 4, 6, 8
</p>

<p>
There are 4 values.
The two middle positions are the 2nd and 3rd:
</p>

<p>
2, <b>4, 6</b>, 8
</p>

<p>
Median:
</p>

<p>
(4 + 6) ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= 10 ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= <b>5</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Find the median of:
</p>

<p><b>3, 9, 5, 7, 2, 10</b></p>

<p>
Arrange:
</p>

<p>
2, 3, 5, 7, 9, 10
</p>

<p>
The two middle values are 5 and 7.
</p>

<p>
Median = (5 + 7) ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= 12 ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= <b>6</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Find the median of:
</p>

<p><b>12, 4, 8, 20, 10, 6, 14, 16</b></p>

<p>
Arrange:
</p>

<p>
4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16, 20
</p>

<p>
The two middle values are 10 and 12.
</p>

<p>
Median = (10 + 12) ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= 22 ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= <b>11</b>
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Do not choose one of the two middle values.
Average the two.
</p>
', 1),
  (224, 224, '
<h2>Median from an Ungrouped Frequency Table</h2>

<p>
A frequency table may represent many repeated observations.
Instead of writing every observation separately, we can use
<cite>cumulative frequency</cite> to locate the middle observation.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Value (x)</th><th>Frequency (f)</th><th>Cumulative Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>3</td><td>5</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>2</td><td>7</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Total frequency:
</p>

<p>
2 + 3 + 2 = <b>7</b>
</p>

<p>
The seven observations represented are:
</p>

<p>
2, 2, 4, 4, 4, 6, 6
</p>

<p>
Median position:
</p>

<p>
(7 + 1) ÷ 2 = <b>4th position</b>
</p>

<p>
The 4th observation is 4.
</p>

<p><b>Median = 4</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th><th>CF</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>6</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>2</td><td>8</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
There are 8 observations.
</p>

<p>
The two middle positions are:
</p>

<p>
8 ÷ 2 = 4th
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
4 + 1 = 5th
</p>

<p>
The 4th and 5th observations are both 3.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Median = 3</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th><th>CF</th></tr>
<tr><td>10</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>20</td><td>2</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>30</td><td>4</td><td>7</td></tr>
<tr><td>40</td><td>2</td><td>9</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Total frequency = 9.
</p>

<p>
Median position:
</p>

<p>
(9 + 1) ÷ 2 = <b>5th position</b>
</p>

<p>
The cumulative frequency reaches 7 at x = 30.
Therefore the 5th observation is 30.
</p>

<p><b>Median = 30</b></p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Cumulative frequency tells us where each value finishes in the
ordered dataset.
That lets us locate the middle without expanding the whole table.
</p>
', 1),
  (225, 225, '
<h2>Finding the Median Position</h2>

<p>
Before finding a median, we need to know where the middle lies.
The method depends on whether the number of observations is odd or
even.
</p>

<h3>Odd number of observations</h3>

<p>
If n is odd:
</p>

<p>
<b>Median position = (n + 1) ÷ 2</b>
</p>

<p>
Example:
</p>

<p>
n = 7
</p>

<p>
(7 + 1) ÷ 2 = 4
</p>

<p>
So the median is the <b>4th observation</b>.
</p>

<h3>Even number of observations</h3>

<p>
If n is even, there are two middle positions:
</p>

<p>
<b>n ÷ 2</b>
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
<b>(n ÷ 2) + 1</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
There are 9 observations.
</p>

<p>
Median position:
</p>

<p>
(9 + 1) ÷ 2 = <b>5th</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
There are 10 observations.
</p>

<p>
First middle position:
</p>

<p>
10 ÷ 2 = <b>5th</b>
</p>

<p>
Second middle position:
</p>

<p>
5 + 1 = <b>6th</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore the median is the average of the 5th and 6th observations.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
There are 12 observations.
</p>

<p>
First middle position:
</p>

<p>
12 ÷ 2 = <b>6th</b>
</p>

<p>
Second middle position:
</p>

<p>
6 + 1 = <b>7th</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore the median is:
</p>

<p>
<b>(6th value + 7th value) ÷ 2</b>
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
A median position is not a data value.
It tells you <b>where to look</b> in the ordered data.
</p>
', 1),
  (226, 226, '
<h2>Median of Grouped Data</h2>

<p>
When data is grouped into class intervals, we do not know the exact
value of every observation.
</p>

<p>
We therefore cannot simply point to one exact observation.
Instead, we first identify the <b>median class</b>.
</p>

<h3>Step 1: Find total frequency</h3>

<p>
Add all frequencies.
</p>

<h3>Step 2: Find the middle position</h3>

<p>
For an odd total frequency:
</p>

<p>
<b>(n + 1) ÷ 2</b>
</p>

<p>
For an even total frequency, identify the two middle positions.
</p>

<h3>Step 3: Use cumulative frequency</h3>

<p>
Find the class containing the middle observation.
That class is the median class.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Frequency</th><th>Cumulative Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>0–9</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>4</td><td>6</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>5</td><td>11</td></tr>
<tr><td>30–39</td><td>3</td><td>14</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Total frequency:
</p>

<p>
2 + 4 + 5 + 3 = <b>14</b>
</p>

<p>
The middle positions are:
</p>

<p>
14 ÷ 2 = <b>7th</b>
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
8th
</p>

<p>
Look at the cumulative frequencies:
</p>

<ul>
<li>0–9 ends at observation 2.</li>
<li>10–19 ends at observation 6.</li>
<li>20–29 ends at observation 11.</li>
</ul>

<p>
Therefore both the 7th and 8th observations lie in:
</p>

<p><b>20–29</b></p>

<p>
So the median class is <b>20–29</b>.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
For grouped data, identifying the median class is different from
finding the exact median value.
</p>

<p>
The grouped table does not tell us the exact individual observations
inside the class.
</p>
', 1),
  (227, 227, '
<h2>Identifying the Median Class</h2>

<p>
The <b>median class</b> is the class interval containing the middle
observation or observations.
</p>

<p>
The key tool is <b>cumulative frequency</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Class</th><th>f</th><th>CF</th></tr>
<tr><td>0–9</td><td>4</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>6</td><td>10</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>3</td><td>13</td></tr>
<tr><td>30–39</td><td>2</td><td>15</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Total frequency = 15.
</p>

<p>
Median position:
</p>

<p>
(15 + 1) ÷ 2 = <b>8th</b>
</p>

<p>
The cumulative frequency reaches:
</p>

<ul>
<li>4 after 0–9</li>
<li>10 after 10–19</li>
</ul>

<p>
Therefore the 8th observation lies in:
</p>

<p><b>10–19</b></p>

<p>
So the median class is 10–19.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Class</th><th>f</th><th>CF</th></tr>
<tr><td>5–9</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>10–14</td><td>3</td><td>5</td></tr>
<tr><td>15–19</td><td>7</td><td>12</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–24</td><td>4</td><td>16</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Total = 16.
</p>

<p>
Middle positions:
</p>

<p>
8th and 9th.
</p>

<p>
The cumulative frequency is 5 after 10–14 and 12 after 15–19.
</p>

<p>
Therefore both middle observations are in:
</p>

<p><b>15–19</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose the cumulative frequencies are:
</p>

<p>
3, 8, 14, 20.
</p>

<p>
If the median position is the 10th observation, locate the first
cumulative frequency that is at least 10.
</p>

<p>
The first value at least 10 is 14.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the median lies in the <b>third class</b>.
</p>

<h3>Key rule</h3>

<p>
Find the median position, then find the <b>first cumulative frequency
that reaches or passes that position</b>.
</p>
', 1),
  (228, 228, '
<h2>Using Cumulative Frequency to Locate the Median</h2>

<p>
Cumulative frequency is a running total.
It tells us how many observations have been counted up to a
particular value or class.
</p>

<p>
This makes it useful for locating the median.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th><th>CF</th></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>3</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>10</td><td>2</td><td>5</td></tr>
<tr><td>15</td><td>4</td><td>9</td></tr>
<tr><td>20</td><td>3</td><td>12</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Total number of observations:
</p>

<p>
n = 12
</p>

<p>
The middle positions are:
</p>

<p>
12 ÷ 2 = <b>6th</b>
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
7th
</p>

<p>
Look at the cumulative frequencies:
</p>

<ul>
<li>5 reaches observation 3.</li>
<li>10 reaches observation 5.</li>
<li>15 reaches observation 9.</li>
</ul>

<p>
Therefore both the 6th and 7th observations are 15.
</p>

<p>
Median = (15 + 15) ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= <b>15</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th><th>CF</th></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>3</td><td>5</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>5</td><td>10</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
There are 10 observations.
</p>

<p>
Middle positions = 5th and 6th.
</p>

<p>
The 5th observation is 4.
</p>

<p>
The 6th observation is 6.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
Median = (4 + 6) ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= <b>5</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th><th>CF</th></tr>
<tr><td>10</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>20</td><td>5</td><td>7</td></tr>
<tr><td>30</td><td>1</td><td>8</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
There are 8 observations.
</p>

<p>
Middle positions:
</p>

<p>
4th and 5th.
</p>

<p>
Both lie within the cumulative frequency of 7 at x = 20.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Median = 20</b></p>
', 1),
  (229, 229, '
<h2>Comparing Median and Mean</h2>

<p>
The <b>mean</b> and <b>median</b> are both measures of central
tendency, but they are calculated differently.
</p>

<h3>Mean</h3>

<p>
The mean uses <b>every value</b> in the dataset.
</p>

<p>
<b>Mean = Total of values ÷ Number of values</b>
</p>

<h3>Median</h3>

<p>
The median depends on the <b>middle position</b> after the data has
been ordered.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>2, 4, 6, 8, 10</b></p>

<p>
Mean:
</p>

<p>
(2 + 4 + 6 + 8 + 10) ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= 30 ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= <b>6</b>
</p>

<p>
Median:
</p>

<p>
The middle value is <b>6</b>.
</p>

<p>
Here:
</p>

<p>
<b>Mean = Median = 6</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>2, 3, 4, 5, 26</b></p>

<p>
Mean:
</p>

<p>
(2 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 26) ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= 40 ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= <b>8</b>
</p>

<p>
Median:
</p>

<p>
The middle value is <b>4</b>.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
Mean = 8
</p>

<p>
Median = 4
</p>

<p>
The large value 26 increases the mean because the mean uses every
value.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>5, 6, 7, 8, 100</b></p>

<p>
Mean:
</p>

<p>
(5 + 6 + 7 + 8 + 100) ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= 126 ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= <b>25.2</b>
</p>

<p>
Median:
</p>

<p>
The middle value is <b>7</b>.
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
Mean = 25.2
</p>

<p>
Median = 7
</p>

<h3>Important idea</h3>

<p>
A very large or very small value can pull the mean toward itself.
The median is based on position, so it is less affected by an
extreme value.
</p>
', 1),
  (230, 230, '
<h2>Choosing Between Mean and Median</h2>

<p>
Both the mean and median describe the centre of a dataset.
However, they do not always respond to data in the same way.
</p>

<h3>When the mean is useful</h3>

<p>
The mean is useful when we want to use <b>all the observations</b>
in the calculation and there are no extreme values causing a
misleading result.
</p>

<h3>When the median is useful</h3>

<p>
The median is useful when the dataset contains an unusually large
or unusually small value that could pull the mean away from the
centre of most observations.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Consider the test marks:
</p>

<p><b>45, 48, 50, 52, 55</b></p>

<p>
Mean:
</p>

<p>
(45 + 48 + 50 + 52 + 55) ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= 250 ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= <b>50</b>
</p>

<p>
Median = <b>50</b>
</p>

<p>
The two measures agree.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Consider monthly values:
</p>

<p><b>10, 11, 12, 13, 100</b></p>

<p>
Mean:
</p>

<p>
(10 + 11 + 12 + 13 + 100) ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= 146 ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= <b>29.2</b>
</p>

<p>
Median = <b>12</b>
</p>

<p>
Most values are near 10–13, but the mean has been pulled upward
by 100.
</p>

<p>
The median better represents the centre of the ordinary values in
this particular dataset.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose five observations are:
</p>

<p><b>20, 21, 22, 23, 24</b></p>

<p>
Mean:
</p>

<p>
110 ÷ 5 = <b>22</b>
</p>

<p>
Median = <b>22</b>
</p>

<p>
There is no extreme value pulling the centre away from the rest,
so the mean gives a good description of the centre.
</p>

<h3>Decision rule</h3>

<p>
Do not automatically say that the median is always better than the
mean.
</p>

<p>
Instead ask:
</p>

<ol>
<li>Are there extreme values?</li>
<li>Do those values strongly affect the mean?</li>
<li>Which measure better represents the centre of this particular
dataset?</li>
</ol>

<p>
The correct choice depends on the data.
</p>
', 1),
  (231, 231, '
<h2>Meaning of the Mode</h2>

<p>
The <b>mode</b> is the value that occurs <b>most frequently</b>
in a dataset.
</p>

<p>
In other words, the mode is the value with the highest frequency.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Find the mode of:
</p>

<p><b>2, 4, 4, 5, 7</b></p>

<p>
Count how many times each value occurs:
</p>

<ul>
<li>2 occurs once.</li>
<li>4 occurs twice.</li>
<li>5 occurs once.</li>
<li>7 occurs once.</li>
</ul>

<p>
The value occurring most often is 4.
</p>

<p><b>Mode = 4</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Find the mode of:
</p>

<p><b>3, 6, 6, 8, 6, 10</b></p>

<p>
The value 6 occurs three times.
</p>

<p>
The other values occur only once.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Mode = 6</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Find the mode of:
</p>

<p><b>5, 7, 8, 7, 9, 7, 10</b></p>

<p>
Count the occurrences:
</p>

<ul>
<li>5 → 1</li>
<li>7 → 3</li>
<li>8 → 1</li>
<li>9 → 1</li>
<li>10 → 1</li>
</ul>

<p>
The highest frequency is 3.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Mode = 7</b></p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
The mode is not necessarily the largest value.
</p>

<p>
It is the value that occurs <b>most often</b>.
</p>

<p>
For example, in:
</p>

<p><b>2, 2, 3, 4, 10</b></p>

<p>
10 is the largest value, but 2 is the mode.
</p>
', 1),
  (232, 232, '
<h2>Finding the Mode from Raw Data</h2>

<p>
Raw data is data listed as individual observations rather than in a
frequency table.
</p>

<p>
To find the mode:
</p>

<ol>
<li>Look at the values.</li>
<li>Count how many times each repeated value occurs.</li>
<li>Identify the value with the greatest frequency.</li>
</ol>

<p>
You do <b>not</b> need to calculate a mean or arrange the data to
find the mode.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Find the mode:
</p>

<p><b>4, 2, 7, 4, 9, 4, 6</b></p>

<p>
Count:
</p>

<ul>
<li>2 → 1</li>
<li>4 → 3</li>
<li>6 → 1</li>
<li>7 → 1</li>
<li>9 → 1</li>
</ul>

<p>
The highest frequency is 3.
</p>

<p><b>Mode = 4</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Find the mode:
</p>

<p><b>8, 3, 8, 5, 6, 3, 8</b></p>

<p>
8 occurs three times.
</p>

<p>
3 occurs twice.
</p>

<p>
The other values occur once.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Mode = 8</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Find the mode:
</p>

<p><b>10, 12, 10, 15, 12, 10, 18, 12</b></p>

<p>
Count:
</p>

<ul>
<li>10 → 3</li>
<li>12 → 3</li>
<li>15 → 1</li>
<li>18 → 1</li>
</ul>

<p>
Two values have the same highest frequency.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the dataset has <b>two modes</b>:
</p>

<p><b>10 and 12</b></p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
A dataset can have:
</p>

<ul>
<li>one mode,</li>
<li>more than one mode, or</li>
<li>no mode if every value occurs equally often.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (233, 233, '
<h2>Mode from an Ungrouped Frequency Table</h2>

<p>
In a frequency table, the mode is the value with the
<b>highest frequency</b>.
</p>

<p>
This makes finding the mode easier because the frequencies have
already been counted.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Value (x)</th><th>Frequency (f)</th></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>5</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>2</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The frequencies are:
</p>

<p>
3, 5 and 2.
</p>

<p>
The highest frequency is 5.
</p>

<p>
That frequency belongs to x = 4.
</p>

<p><b>Mode = 4</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th></tr>
<tr><td>10</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>20</td><td>7</td></tr>
<tr><td>30</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>40</td><td>3</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The largest frequency is 7.
</p>

<p>
Frequency 7 belongs to x = 20.
</p>

<p><b>Mode = 20</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>6</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>6</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>2</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The highest frequency is 6.
</p>

<p>
Both x = 2 and x = 3 have frequency 6.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the dataset has two modes:
</p>

<p><b>2 and 3</b></p>

<h3>Key rule</h3>

<p>
Do not choose the largest value in the x-column.
</p>

<p>
Look for the <b>largest frequency</b>, then read the corresponding
value.
</p>
', 1),
  (234, 234, '
<h2>Identifying the Modal Class</h2>

<p>
When data is grouped into class intervals, we cannot identify the
exact mode from the grouped table because the individual values
inside each class are unknown.
</p>

<p>
Instead, we identify the <b>modal class</b>.
</p>

<p>
The modal class is the class interval with the
<b>highest frequency</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>0–9</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>8</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>5</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The highest frequency is 8.
</p>

<p>
Frequency 8 belongs to class 10–19.
</p>

<p><b>Modal class = 10–19</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>5–9</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>10–14</td><td>6</td></tr>
<tr><td>15–19</td><td>9</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–24</td><td>3</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The highest frequency is 9.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Modal class = 15–19</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>7</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>11</td></tr>
<tr><td>30–39</td><td>11</td></tr>
<tr><td>40–49</td><td>4</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The highest frequency is 11.
</p>

<p>
Two classes have frequency 11:
</p>

<p>
20–29 and 30–39.
</p>

<p>
Therefore there are two modal classes.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
<b>Ungrouped data:</b> identify the mode itself.
</p>

<p>
<b>Grouped data:</b> identify the modal class.
</p>

<p>
Do not invent an exact mode when the grouped table does not provide
the individual observations.
</p>
', 1),
  (235, 235, '
<h2>Comparing Mean, Median and Mode</h2>

<p>
The <b>mean</b>, <b>median</b> and <b>mode</b> all describe the
centre or typical behaviour of a dataset, but they answer different
questions.
</p>

<h3>Mean</h3>

<p>
The mean is the total of all values divided by the number of values.
</p>

<p>
<b>Mean = Σx ÷ n</b>
</p>

<h3>Median</h3>

<p>
The median is the middle value after the data has been arranged.
</p>

<h3>Mode</h3>

<p>
The mode is the value that occurs most frequently.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>2, 3, 3, 4, 8</b></p>

<p>
Mean:
</p>

<p>
(2 + 3 + 3 + 4 + 8) ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= 20 ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= <b>4</b>
</p>

<p>
Median:
</p>

<p>
The middle value is <b>3</b>.
</p>

<p>
Mode:
</p>

<p>
3 occurs twice, more than any other value.
</p>

<p>
<b>Mode = 3</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
Mean = 4
</p>

<p>
Median = 3
</p>

<p>
Mode = 3
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>4, 5, 5, 5, 6</b></p>

<p>
Mean:
</p>

<p>
(4 + 5 + 5 + 5 + 6) ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= 25 ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= <b>5</b>
</p>

<p>
Median = <b>5</b>
</p>

<p>
Mode = <b>5</b>
</p>

<p>
All three measures are equal.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>1, 2, 2, 3, 12</b></p>

<p>
Mean:
</p>

<p>
(1 + 2 + 2 + 3 + 12) ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= 20 ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= <b>4</b>
</p>

<p>
Median = <b>2</b>
</p>

<p>
Mode = <b>2</b>
</p>

<p>
The extreme value 12 raises the mean.
</p>

<h3>What each measure asks</h3>

<ul>
<li><b>Mean:</b> What is the equal-share value?</li>
<li><b>Median:</b> What is the middle value?</li>
<li><b>Mode:</b> What value occurs most often?</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (236, 236, '
<h2>Meaning of Range</h2>

<p>
The <b>range</b> measures how far apart the smallest and largest
values in a dataset are.
</p>

<p>
The formula is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Range = Largest value − Smallest value</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Find the range of:
</p>

<p><b>3, 5, 8, 10, 12</b></p>

<p>
Largest value = 12
</p>

<p>
Smallest value = 3
</p>

<p>
Range = 12 − 3
</p>

<p>
= <b>9</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Find the range of:
</p>

<p><b>18, 7, 12, 25, 10</b></p>

<p>
Largest value = 25
</p>

<p>
Smallest value = 7
</p>

<p>
Range = 25 − 7
</p>

<p>
= <b>18</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Find the range of:
</p>

<p><b>42, 42, 42, 42</b></p>

<p>
Largest = 42
</p>

<p>
Smallest = 42
</p>

<p>
Range = 42 − 42
</p>

<p>
= <b>0</b>
</p>

<p>
A range of zero means all the values are the same.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Range is a measure of <b>spread</b>, not a measure of the centre.
</p>

<p>
Mean, median and mode describe central tendency.
Range tells us how far apart the extreme values are.
</p>
', 1),
  (237, 237, '
<h2>Finding Range from Raw Data</h2>

<p>
For raw data, finding the range requires only two values:
</p>

<p>
<b>the largest value</b> and <b>the smallest value</b>.
</p>

<p>
Then subtract:
</p>

<p>
<b>Range = Largest − Smallest</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Find the range:
</p>

<p><b>12, 5, 18, 7, 10</b></p>

<p>
Largest = 18
</p>

<p>
Smallest = 5
</p>

<p>
Range = 18 − 5
</p>

<p>
= <b>13</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Find the range:
</p>

<p><b>30, 14, 22, 9, 41, 17</b></p>

<p>
Largest = 41
</p>

<p>
Smallest = 9
</p>

<p>
Range = 41 − 9
</p>

<p>
= <b>32</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Find the range:
</p>

<p><b>−4, 2, −1, 7, 5</b></p>

<p>
Largest = 7
</p>

<p>
Smallest = −4
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
Range = 7 − (−4)
</p>

<p>
= 7 + 4
</p>

<p>
= <b>11</b>
</p>

<h3>Common mistake with negative values</h3>

<p>
If the smallest value is negative, subtracting it means adding its
positive amount.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
8 − (−3) = 8 + 3 = 11
</p>
', 1),
  (238, 238, '
<h2>Range from a Frequency Table</h2>

<p>
In an ungrouped frequency table, the range is determined from the
smallest and largest <b>values</b>, not from their frequencies.
</p>

<p>
The formula remains:
</p>

<p>
<b>Range = Largest value − Smallest value</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>9</td><td>2</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Smallest value = 2
</p>

<p>
Largest value = 9
</p>

<p>
Range = 9 − 2
</p>

<p>
= <b>7</b>
</p>

<p>
Notice that the frequencies do not enter the range calculation.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th></tr>
<tr><td>10</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>15</td><td>6</td></tr>
<tr><td>20</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>30</td><td>1</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Smallest = 10
</p>

<p>
Largest = 30
</p>

<p>
Range = 30 − 10
</p>

<p>
= <b>20</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th></tr>
<tr><td>−3</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>5</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>3</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Largest = 6
</p>

<p>
Smallest = −3
</p>

<p>
Range = 6 − (−3)
</p>

<p>
= 6 + 3
</p>

<p>
= <b>9</b>
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
The frequency tells us <b>how many</b> observations have a value.
</p>

<p>
The range uses <b>which values</b> are smallest and largest.
</p>
', 1),
  (239, 239, '
<h2>Comparing Two Data Sets Using Range</h2>

<p>
The range can be used to compare the spread of two datasets.
</p>

<p>
A <b>larger range</b> means the data stretches farther between its
smallest and largest values.
</p>

<p>
A <b>smaller range</b> means the extreme values are closer together.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Dataset A:
</p>

<p><b>4, 6, 8, 10</b></p>

<p>
Range A:
</p>

<p>
10 − 4 = <b>6</b>
</p>

<p>
Dataset B:
</p>

<p><b>2, 6, 8, 14</b></p>

<p>
Range B:
</p>

<p>
14 − 2 = <b>12</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore B has the greater range.
</p>

<p>
B has greater spread according to the range.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Dataset A:
</p>

<p><b>10, 12, 14, 16</b></p>

<p>
Range = 16 − 10 = <b>6</b>
</p>

<p>
Dataset B:
</p>

<p><b>20, 21, 22, 23</b></p>

<p>
Range = 23 − 20 = <b>3</b>
</p>

<p>
Dataset A has the larger range.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Dataset A:
</p>

<p><b>5, 5, 5, 5</b></p>

<p>
Range = 0.
</p>

<p>
Dataset B:
</p>

<p><b>2, 5, 8, 11</b></p>

<p>
Range = 11 − 2 = 9.
</p>

<p>
Dataset B has greater spread.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Range only compares the smallest and largest values.
It does not tell us how the observations are distributed between
those extremes.
</p>
', 1),
  (240, 240, '
<h2>Effect of an Extreme Value on Range</h2>

<p>
The range depends entirely on the smallest and largest values.
Therefore, an extreme value can change the range dramatically.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Original data:
</p>

<p><b>4, 5, 6, 7, 8</b></p>

<p>
Largest = 8
</p>

<p>
Smallest = 4
</p>

<p>
Range = 8 − 4 = <b>4</b>
</p>

<p>
Now add an extreme value of 20:
</p>

<p><b>4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 20</b></p>

<p>
Largest = 20
</p>

<p>
Smallest = 4
</p>

<p>
New range = 20 − 4 = <b>16</b>
</p>

<p>
The range increased from 4 to 16.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Original data:
</p>

<p><b>10, 12, 14, 16</b></p>

<p>
Range = 16 − 10 = <b>6</b>
</p>

<p>
Add −5:
</p>

<p><b>−5, 10, 12, 14, 16</b></p>

<p>
New range:
</p>

<p>
16 − (−5)
</p>

<p>
= 16 + 5
</p>

<p>
= <b>21</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose the smallest value and largest value remain unchanged.
Adding a value that lies between them does not change the range.
</p>

<p>
Original:
</p>

<p><b>5, 10, 15</b></p>

<p>
Range = 15 − 5 = <b>10</b>
</p>

<p>
Add 12:
</p>

<p><b>5, 10, 12, 15</b></p>

<p>
New range = 15 − 5 = <b>10</b>
</p>

<p>
The range did not change.
</p>

<h3>The rule</h3>

<p>
Adding a value changes the range only if it becomes the new
smallest or new largest value.
</p>

<p>
If the new value lies between the existing extremes, the range
stays the same.
</p>
', 1),
  (241, 241, '
<h2>Reading a Bar Graph</h2>

<p>
A <b>bar graph</b> represents data using separate rectangular bars.
The height of each bar shows the frequency or quantity for that category.
</p>

<p>
The categories are normally written along the horizontal axis,
while the frequency or quantity is shown on the vertical axis.
</p>

<h3>How to read a bar graph</h3>

<ol>
  <li>Identify what the horizontal axis represents.</li>
  <li>Identify what the vertical axis represents.</li>
  <li>Read the scale on the vertical axis.</li>
  <li>Find the bar for the required category.</li>
  <li>Read the height of that bar.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose a bar graph shows the number of books read by four learners:
</p>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Learner</th><th>Books</th></tr>
<tr><td>A</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>B</td><td>7</td></tr>
<tr><td>C</td><td>5</td></tr>
<tr><td>D</td><td>3</td></tr>
</table>

<p><b>Question:</b> How many books did learner B read?</p>

<p>
The bar for B reaches 7 on the vertical scale.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
<br>
<b>Books read = 7</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A bar graph shows the number of learners choosing four subjects:
</p>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Subject</th><th>Learners</th></tr>
<tr><td>Math</td><td>18</td></tr>
<tr><td>English</td><td>15</td></tr>
<tr><td>Biology</td><td>12</td></tr>
<tr><td>Physics</td><td>10</td></tr>
</table>

<p><b>Question:</b> Which subject has the highest number of learners?</p>

<p>
Compare the frequencies:
</p>

<p>
18, 15, 12, 10
</p>

<p>
The greatest value is 18.
Therefore the tallest bar represents <b>Math</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A bar graph has the following values:
</p>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Day</th><th>Visitors</th></tr>
<tr><td>Monday</td><td>20</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tuesday</td><td>35</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wednesday</td><td>25</td></tr>
</table>

<p><b>Question:</b> How many more visitors came on Tuesday than Monday?</p>

<p>
Tuesday = 35
<br>
Monday = 20
</p>

<p>
Difference:
</p>

<p>
35 − 20 = <b>15</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore, Tuesday had <b>15 more visitors</b>.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
When reading a bar graph, do not guess the value from the appearance of the bar.
Read the scale carefully and match the top of the bar with the vertical axis.
</p>
