<h2>Constructing a Bar Graph</h2>

<p>
A bar graph is constructed by turning the values in a table into bars.
Each category gets its own separate bar.
</p>

<h3>Steps</h3>

<ol>
  <li>Write the categories on the horizontal axis.</li>
  <li>Write the frequency or quantity on the vertical axis.</li>
  <li>Choose a suitable scale.</li>
  <li>Draw the axes.</li>
  <li>Draw one bar for each category.</li>
  <li>Make each bar reach the correct value.</li>
  <li>Keep equal spaces between separate bars.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Fruit</th><th>Number sold</th></tr>
<tr><td>Apples</td><td>10</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bananas</td><td>15</td></tr>
<tr><td>Oranges</td><td>8</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The largest value is 15.
A convenient vertical scale is:
</p>

<p>
0, 5, 10, 15
</p>

<p>
The bars therefore have heights:
</p>

<p>
Apples = 10
<br>
Bananas = 15
<br>
Oranges = 8
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>House</th><th>Learners</th></tr>
<tr><td>Red</td><td>20</td></tr>
<tr><td>Blue</td><td>30</td></tr>
<tr><td>Green</td><td>25</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The largest value is 30.
</p>

<p>
A suitable scale is:
</p>

<p>
0, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30
</p>

<p>
The Blue bar must reach 30,
the Green bar must reach 25,
and the Red bar must reach 20.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Day</th><th>Visitors</th></tr>
<tr><td>Monday</td><td>6</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tuesday</td><td>12</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wednesday</td><td>9</td></tr>
<tr><td>Thursday</td><td>15</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The largest frequency is 15.
</p>

<p>
A scale of 0, 5, 10, 15 is suitable.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the four bars must reach:
</p>

<p>
Monday → 6
<br>
Tuesday → 12
<br>
Wednesday → 9
<br>
Thursday → 15
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
A bar graph must have a clear scale.
If the scale changes irregularly, the graph can give a false impression of the data.
</p>
  ', 1),
  (243, 243, '
<h2>Reading a Histogram</h2>

<p>
A <b>histogram</b> represents grouped numerical data using adjoining bars.
The classes are placed along the horizontal axis and the frequencies are shown vertically.
</p>

<p>
Unlike the separated bars of a bar graph, histogram bars <b>touch</b>
because the class intervals represent continuous or consecutive numerical ranges.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>0–9</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>7</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>5</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
If the histogram is drawn from this table:
</p>

<ul>
<li>The 0–9 bar has height 4.</li>
<li>The 10–19 bar has height 7.</li>
<li>The 20–29 bar has height 5.</li>
</ul>

<p>
The tallest bar is therefore the class <b>10–19</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A histogram has class frequencies:
</p>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>9</td></tr>
<tr><td>30–39</td><td>6</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The question asks:
<b>How many observations lie in the class 20–29?</b>
</p>

<p>
Read the height of the 20–29 bar.
</p>

<p>
Height = 9
</p>

<p>
Therefore, <b>9 observations</b> lie in that class.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose the histogram represents ages:
</p>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Age class</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>10–14</td><td>5</td></tr>
<tr><td>15–19</td><td>8</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–24</td><td>4</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The most frequent age class is found by locating the tallest bar.
</p>

<p>
The frequencies are:
</p>

<p>
5, 8, 4
</p>

<p>
The largest is 8.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the modal class is <b>15–19</b>.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
When reading a histogram, connect the height of a bar to the class interval directly below it.
</p>
  ', 1),
  (244, 244, '
<h2>Constructing a Histogram</h2>

<p>
A histogram is constructed from grouped numerical data.
The class intervals form the horizontal axis and the frequencies determine the heights.
</p>

<h3>Steps</h3>

<ol>
  <li>Write the class intervals on the horizontal axis.</li>
  <li>Write frequency on the vertical axis.</li>
  <li>Choose a suitable frequency scale.</li>
  <li>Convert class limits to boundaries when necessary.</li>
  <li>Draw adjoining bars.</li>
  <li>Make each bar reach the correct frequency.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>7</td></tr>
<tr><td>30–39</td><td>5</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
For continuous histogram boundaries:
</p>

<p>
10–19 becomes 9.5–19.5
<br>
20–29 becomes 19.5–29.5
<br>
30–39 becomes 29.5–39.5
</p>

<p>
The bars must have heights:
</p>

<p>
4, 7 and 5 respectively.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Height (cm)</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>140–149</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>150–159</td><td>8</td></tr>
<tr><td>160–169</td><td>6</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The class 150–159 has the greatest frequency.
</p>

<p>
Therefore its bar must be the tallest and must reach frequency 8.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Marks</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>0–9</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>6</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>10</td></tr>
<tr><td>30–39</td><td>7</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The largest frequency is 10.
A suitable vertical scale is:
</p>

<p>
0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10
</p>

<p>
The bars therefore have heights:
</p>

<p>
2, 6, 10 and 7.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Histogram bars touch.
There should not be spaces between adjacent class intervals.
</p>
  ', 1),
  (245, 245, '
<h2>Bar Graph versus Histogram</h2>

<p>
A bar graph and a histogram both use rectangular bars,
but they are used for different kinds of data.
</p>

<h3>Bar Graph</h3>

<p>
A bar graph is normally used for <b>categories</b>.
The categories are separate, so spaces are left between the bars.
</p>

<p>
Example:
</p>

<p>
Subjects → Mathematics, English, Biology, Physics
</p>

<p>
These are separate categories.
</p>

<h3>Histogram</h3>

<p>
A histogram is used for <b>grouped numerical data</b>.
The class intervals are consecutive, so the bars touch.
</p>

<p>
Example:
</p>

<p>
10–19, 20–29, 30–39
</p>

<p>
These intervals represent numerical ranges.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A school records the number of learners who prefer:
</p>

<p>
Football, Basketball, Volleyball and Athletics.
</p>

<p>
These are categories.
</p>

<p>
Therefore a <b>bar graph</b> is appropriate.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A teacher records marks in classes:
</p>

<p>
0–9, 10–19, 20–29, 30–39.
</p>

<p>
These are numerical intervals.
</p>

<p>
Therefore a <b>histogram</b> is appropriate.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A graph contains separate bars for:
</p>

<p>
Red House = 20
<br>
Blue House = 25
<br>
Green House = 18
</p>

<p>
House names are categories.
The bars are therefore separated.
This is a <b>bar graph</b>.
</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr>
<th>Bar Graph</th>
<th>Histogram</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Categorical data</td>
<td>Grouped numerical data</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Bars separated</td>
<td>Bars touch</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Separate categories</td>
<td>Numerical intervals</td>
</tr>
</table>
  ', 1),
  (246, 246, '
<h2>Reading a Frequency Polygon</h2>

<p>
A <b>frequency polygon</b> represents grouped data using points plotted at class midpoints.
The points are then joined with straight lines.
</p>

<p>
To read a frequency polygon, connect each plotted point to:
</p>

<p>
<b>class midpoint → frequency</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Midpoint</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>0–9</td><td>4.5</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>14.5</td><td>7</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>24.5</td><td>5</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The plotted points are:
</p>

<p>
(4.5, 3), (14.5, 7), (24.5, 5)
</p>

<p>
Therefore, at midpoint 14.5, the frequency is 7.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A point on a frequency polygon is at:
</p>

<p>
(35, 9)
</p>

<p>
The first coordinate is the class midpoint.
The second coordinate is the frequency.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
Midpoint = 35
<br>
Frequency = 9
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Three plotted points have frequencies:
</p>

<p>
4, 10 and 6.
</p>

<p>
The highest point has frequency 10.
Therefore the class represented by that midpoint has the greatest frequency.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
In a frequency polygon:
</p>

<p>
<b>Horizontal position → class midpoint</b>
<br>
<b>Vertical position → frequency</b>
</p>
  ', 1),
  (247, 247, '
<h2>Constructing a Frequency Polygon</h2>

<p>
A frequency polygon is constructed by finding the midpoint of each class,
plotting the midpoint against its frequency, and joining the points.
</p>

<h3>Steps</h3>

<ol>
  <li>Write the class intervals.</li>
  <li>Find the midpoint of every class.</li>
  <li>Use midpoint as the horizontal coordinate.</li>
  <li>Use frequency as the vertical coordinate.</li>
  <li>Plot the points.</li>
  <li>Join the points using straight lines.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>0–9</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>7</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>5</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Find each midpoint:
</p>

<p>
0–9:
<br>
(0 + 9) ÷ 2 = 4.5
</p>

<p>
10–19:
<br>
(10 + 19) ÷ 2 = 14.5
</p>

<p>
20–29:
<br>
(20 + 29) ÷ 2 = 24.5
</p>

<p>
The points are therefore:
</p>

<p>
(4.5, 4), (14.5, 7), (24.5, 5)
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>30–39</td><td>8</td></tr>
<tr><td>40–49</td><td>6</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Midpoints:
</p>

<p>
20–29 → (20 + 29) ÷ 2 = 24.5
<br>
30–39 → (30 + 39) ÷ 2 = 34.5
<br>
40–49 → (40 + 49) ÷ 2 = 44.5
</p>

<p>
Points:
</p>

<p>
(24.5, 3), (34.5, 8), (44.5, 6)
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>50–59</td><td>5</td></tr>
<tr><td>60–69</td><td>10</td></tr>
<tr><td>70–79</td><td>4</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Midpoints:
</p>

<p>
50–59 → 54.5
<br>
60–69 → 64.5
<br>
70–79 → 74.5
</p>

<p>
Therefore the plotted points are:
</p>

<p>
(54.5, 5), (64.5, 10), (74.5, 4)
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Do not plot the upper or lower class limits as the horizontal coordinate.
The frequency polygon uses the <b>class midpoint</b>.
</p>
  ', 1),
  (248, 248, '
<h2>Interpreting a Frequency Polygon</h2>

<p>
A frequency polygon allows us to identify where frequencies are high or low
and compare the distribution across different class intervals.
</p>

<p>
The important rule is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Higher point = higher frequency.</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Midpoint</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>14.5</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>24.5</td><td>8</td></tr>
<tr><td>34.5</td><td>5</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The highest frequency is 8.
</p>

<p>
It occurs at midpoint 24.5.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the class centred at 24.5 has the greatest frequency.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A frequency polygon has frequencies:
</p>

<p>
2, 5, 11, 7, 3
</p>

<p>
The largest frequency is 11.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the highest point occurs at the midpoint associated with frequency 11.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose two consecutive points have frequencies 12 and 4.
</p>

<p>
The second point is much lower.
</p>

<p>
This means the number of observations in the second class is much smaller than in the first class.
</p>

<p>
Difference:
</p>

<p>
12 − 4 = <b>8</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore the second class has 8 fewer observations.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
A frequency polygon does not tell us the exact individual values.
It shows the frequencies associated with class midpoints.
</p>
  ', 1),
  (249, 249, '
<h2>Comparing Two Data Representations</h2>

<p>
The same dataset can sometimes be represented in different ways.
When comparing two representations, check whether they show the same
frequencies and identify what each representation makes easier to see.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose a grouped table contains:
</p>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>0–9</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>8</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>5</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
A histogram for the data should have bar heights:
</p>

<p>
4, 8 and 5.
</p>

<p>
A frequency polygon for the same data should have points whose vertical
coordinates are also:
</p>

<p>
4, 8 and 5.
</p>

<p>
Therefore both representations show the same frequencies.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Representation A shows frequencies:
</p>

<p>
6, 10, 7
</p>

<p>
Representation B shows frequencies:
</p>

<p>
6, 10, 7
</p>

<p>
The two representations agree because corresponding frequencies are equal.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Representation A shows frequencies:
</p>

<p>
5, 8, 12
</p>

<p>
Representation B shows:
</p>

<p>
5, 7, 12
</p>

<p>
The representations do not agree.
</p>

<p>
The difference is in the middle class:
</p>

<p>
8 − 7 = <b>1</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore one representation has one extra observation in that class.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
When comparing representations, compare corresponding classes and frequencies.
Do not compare only the visual appearance.
</p>
  ', 1),
  (250, 250, '
<h2>Choosing the Appropriate Data Representation</h2>

<p>
Different types of data require different forms of representation.
The choice should depend on what the data are and what we want the graph to show.
</p>

<h3>Bar Graph</h3>

<p>
Use a bar graph when comparing separate categories.
</p>

<p>
Example:
</p>

<p>
Favourite sports:
Football, Athletics, Basketball, Volleyball.
</p>

<p>
These are separate categories.
A bar graph is appropriate.
</p>

<h3>Histogram</h3>

<p>
Use a histogram when numerical data have been grouped into class intervals.
</p>

<p>
Example:
</p>

<p>
Marks:
0–9, 10–19, 20–29, 30–39.
</p>

<p>
A histogram is appropriate.
</p>

<h3>Frequency Polygon</h3>

<p>
Use a frequency polygon to represent grouped frequency data by plotting
frequency against class midpoint and joining the points.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A school wants to show the number of learners in:
</p>

<p>
Form 1, Form 2, Form 3 and Form 4.
</p>

<p>
These are separate categories.
</p>

<p>
Therefore a <b>bar graph</b> is appropriate.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A teacher groups examination marks into:
</p>

<p>
0–9, 10–19, 20–29, 30–39, 40–49.
</p>

<p>
These are numerical class intervals.
</p>

<p>
Therefore a <b>histogram</b> is appropriate.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A teacher wants to show how the frequency changes from one class midpoint
to the next for grouped marks.
</p>

<p>
A <b>frequency polygon</b> is appropriate because it shows the frequencies
as connected points at the class midpoints.
</p>

<h3>Decision rule</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr>
<th>Data</th>
<th>Suitable representation</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Separate categories</td>
<td>Bar graph</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Grouped numerical intervals</td>
<td>Histogram</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Grouped frequencies using class midpoints</td>
<td>Frequency polygon</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Do not choose a graph because it looks attractive.
Choose it because it matches the structure of the data.
</p>
  ', 1);
INSERT INTO public.lesson_versions (id, lesson_id, content, version) VALUES
  (251, 251, '
<h2>Probability Experiment</h2>

<p>
A <b>probability experiment</b> is an action that we perform where the
outcome is not known with certainty before the action is performed.
</p>

<p>
For example, if we toss a coin, we know that the result will be either
<b>Head</b> or <b>Tail</b>, but we do not know which one will occur before
the coin is tossed.
</p>

<h3>What makes an experiment a probability experiment?</h3>

<p>
There are two important ideas:
</p>

<ol>
  <li>We know the possible outcomes.</li>
  <li>We do not know which outcome will occur before performing the experiment.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Example 1: Tossing a coin</h3>

<p>
Suppose we toss a coin once.
</p>

<p>
Before tossing it, we know that the possible outcomes are:
</p>

<p><b>Head or Tail</b></p>

<p>
However, we cannot know with certainty whether the result will be Head
or Tail before tossing the coin.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, tossing a coin is a probability experiment.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Rolling a die</h3>

<p>
Suppose we roll a fair six-sided die once.
</p>

<p>
The possible outcomes are:
</p>

<p>
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6
</p>

<p>
We know these possible outcomes, but we cannot know which number will
appear before rolling the die.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, rolling a die is a probability experiment.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Drawing a ball</h3>

<p>
A bag contains red and blue balls. One ball is drawn without looking.
</p>

<p>
Before drawing, we know the possible colours.
</p>

<p>
But we do not know which colour will actually be drawn.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, drawing a ball is a probability experiment.
</p>

<h3>Important idea</h3>

<p>
A probability experiment does not mean that the result is completely
unknown. We know the possible outcomes; what we do not know beforehand
is which outcome will occur.
</p>
', 1),
  (252, 252, '
<h2>Outcomes of an Experiment</h2>

<p>
An <b>outcome</b> is one possible result of a probability experiment.
</p>

<p>
Before calculating probability, we need to know what outcomes are possible.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: One coin toss</h3>

<p>
If a coin is tossed once, there are two possible outcomes:
</p>

<p>
<b>Head (H)</b> and <b>Tail (T)</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
Outcomes = {H, T}
</p>

<h3>Example 2: One die roll</h3>

<p>
A standard die has six faces.
</p>

<p>
The possible outcomes are:
</p>

<p>
{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}
</p>

<p>
Each number represents one possible result of the experiment.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Drawing a coloured ball</h3>

<p>
A bag contains red, blue and green balls.
One ball is drawn.
</p>

<p>
The possible colour outcomes are:
</p>

<p>
{Red, Blue, Green}
</p>

<p>
The individual balls may be different, but if we are interested only
in colour, these are the outcomes we record.
</p>

<h3>Why identifying outcomes matters</h3>

<p>
Probability calculations depend on knowing what can happen.
If an outcome is forgotten, the probability calculation can become wrong.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, before calculating a probability, ask:
</p>

<p><b>"What are all the possible outcomes?"</b></p>
', 1),
  (253, 253, '
<h2>Recording Experimental Results</h2>

<p>
When we repeat a probability experiment, the results should be recorded
so that we can count how often each outcome occurs.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Tossing a coin</h3>

<p>
Suppose a coin is tossed 10 times and the results are:
</p>

<p>
H, T, H, H, T, T, H, T, H, H
</p>

<p>
Count the results:
</p>

<p>
Heads = 6
</p>

<p>
Tails = 4
</p>

<p>
Total tosses:
</p>

<p>
6 + 4 = 10
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Rolling a die</h3>

<p>
Suppose a die is rolled 12 times and the results are:
</p>

<p>
2, 4, 1, 6, 2, 3, 2, 5, 4, 2, 6, 1
</p>

<p>
Count each outcome:
</p>

<p>
1 → 2 times
</p>

<p>
2 → 4 times
</p>

<p>
3 → 1 time
</p>

<p>
4 → 2 times
</p>

<p>
5 → 1 time
</p>

<p>
6 → 2 times
</p>

<p>
Check the total:
</p>

<p>
2 + 4 + 1 + 2 + 1 + 2 = 12
</p>

<p>
The total frequency must equal the total number of trials.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Why record frequencies?</h3>

<p>
Suppose a coin is tossed 20 times and Head occurs 13 times.
</p>

<p>
The frequency of Head is therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>13</b>
</p>

<p>
The total number of trials is:
</p>

<p>
<b>20</b>
</p>

<p>
These two numbers will later allow us to calculate the experimental
probability of getting Head.
</p>
', 1),
  (254, 254, '
<h2>Calculating Experimental Probability</h2>

<p>
<b>Experimental probability</b> is the probability estimated from results
actually obtained when an experiment is performed.
</p>

<p>
The formula is:
</p>

<p>
<b>
Experimental Probability =
Number of times the outcome occurred
÷
Total number of trials
</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Coin</h3>

<p>
A coin is tossed 20 times.
Heads occur 12 times.
Find the experimental probability of getting Head.
</p>

<p>
Number of Heads = 12
</p>

<p>
Total tosses = 20
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(Head) = 12 ÷ 20
</p>

<p>
P(Head) = 3/5
</p>

<p>
So the experimental probability is <b>3/5</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Die</h3>

<p>
A die is rolled 30 times.
The number 6 appears 4 times.
Find the experimental probability of getting 6.
</p>

<p>
P(6) = 4 ÷ 30
</p>

<p>
P(6) = 2/15
</p>

<p>
Therefore, the experimental probability is <b>2/15</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Coloured balls</h3>

<p>
A ball is selected from a bag 40 times, with the ball replaced each time.
A red ball is obtained 18 times.
Find the experimental probability of selecting red.
</p>

<p>
P(Red) = 18 ÷ 40
</p>

<p>
P(Red) = 9/20
</p>

<p>
Therefore, the experimental probability of red is <b>9/20</b>.
</p>

<h3>The key idea</h3>

<p>
Do not divide by the number of times the outcome could have occurred.
Divide by the <b>total number of trials actually performed</b>.
</p>
', 1),
  (255, 255, '
<h2>Comparing Experimental and Theoretical Probability</h2>

<p>
<b>Theoretical probability</b> is the probability we expect from the
possible outcomes of an experiment.
</p>

<p>
<b>Experimental probability</b> is the probability obtained from actually
performing the experiment.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: One coin</h3>

<p>
For a fair coin, there are two equally likely outcomes:
</p>

<p>
Head and Tail.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
Theoretical P(Head) = 1/2
</p>

<p>
Suppose we toss the coin 10 times and obtain 7 Heads.
</p>

<p>
Experimental P(Head) = 7/10
</p>

<p>
Notice:
</p>

<p>
7/10 ≠ 1/2
</p>

<p>
This does not automatically mean that the experiment is wrong.
The number of trials is small, so the observed results may differ from
the theoretical probability.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: More trials</h3>

<p>
Suppose another experiment uses 100 tosses of the same fair coin and
produces 52 Heads.
</p>

<p>
Experimental probability:
</p>

<p>
P(Head) = 52/100
</p>

<p>
P(Head) = 0.52
</p>

<p>
The theoretical probability is:
</p>

<p>
P(Head) = 0.5
</p>

<p>
The experimental result, 0.52, is now closer to 0.5.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: What happens as trials increase?</h3>

<p>
Imagine repeating the same fair-coin experiment many more times.
The experimental probability will generally tend to get closer to the
theoretical probability.
</p>

<p>
This does not mean that every experiment must produce exactly the
theoretical probability.
</p>

<p>
For example, 10 tosses do not have to produce exactly 5 Heads.
</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>

<p>
<b>Theoretical probability</b> comes from the possible outcomes.
</p>

<p>
<b>Experimental probability</b> comes from actual observations.
</p>

<p>
They can be different, especially when the number of trials is small.
With many trials, experimental results generally become more stable and
tend towards the theoretical probability.
</p>
', 1),
  (256, 256, '
<h2>Complete Probability Space</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>
<p>
A <b>probability space</b> is the complete collection of all possible outcomes
of an experiment.
</p>

<p>
The important word is <b>complete</b>.
We must include every outcome that can happen and must not include an outcome
that cannot happen.
</p>

<h3>WHY DO WE NEED IT?</h3>

<p>
Before finding the probability of an event, we need to know what can happen.
If we leave out a possible outcome, our probability calculation can become wrong.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Tossing one coin</h3>

<p>
A coin has two possible results:
</p>

<p>
<b>H</b> = Head<br>
<b>T</b> = Tail
</p>

<p>
Therefore the complete probability space is:
</p>

<p>
<b>S = {H, T}</b>
</p>

<p>
There are no other possible results in an ordinary coin toss.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Rolling one die</h3>

<p>
A standard die has six faces numbered 1 to 6.
Therefore the possible outcomes are:
</p>

<p>
<b>S = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}</b>
</p>

<p>
We include all six numbers because any of them can appear.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Choosing one letter from A, B and C</h3>

<p>
The possible choices are A, B and C.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>S = {A, B, C}</b>
</p>

<p>
The set contains exactly the possible outcomes.
</p>

<h3>CHECK YOUR THINKING</h3>

<p>
Whenever you are asked to give a probability space, ask:
</p>

<ol>
<li>What experiment is being performed?</li>
<li>What can happen in that experiment?</li>
<li>Have I included every possible outcome?</li>
<li>Have I included anything impossible?</li>
</ol>
