<h2>Probability Space for Two Coin Tosses</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>
<p>
When an experiment is performed twice, the outcome must show the result of
<b>each trial</b> and the order matters.
</p>

<p>
For two coin tosses, we cannot simply write H and T.
We must show what happened on the first toss and what happened on the second toss.
</p>

<h3>Step 1: First toss</h3>

<p>
The first coin toss can produce:
</p>

<p>
H or T
</p>

<h3>Step 2: Second toss</h3>

<p>
The second coin toss can also produce:
</p>

<p>
H or T
</p>

<h3>Step 3: Combine them</h3>

<p>
If the first toss is H, the second toss can be H or T:
</p>

<p>
HH, HT
</p>

<p>
If the first toss is T, the second toss can be H or T:
</p>

<p>
TH, TT
</p>

<p>
Therefore the complete probability space is:
</p>

<p>
<b>S = {HH, HT, TH, TT}</b>
</p>

<h3>Why are HT and TH different?</h3>

<p>
HT means:
</p>

<p>
First toss = H<br>
Second toss = T
</p>

<p>
TH means:
</p>

<p>
First toss = T<br>
Second toss = H
</p>

<p>
The results are in different orders, so they are different outcomes.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Three possible outcomes checked</h3>

<p>
Suppose someone writes:
</p>

<p>
{HH, HT, TT}
</p>

<p>
This is incomplete because <b>TH</b> can also occur.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Building the list systematically</h3>

<p>
Start with H on the first toss:
</p>

<p>
HH, HT
</p>

<p>
Then start with T on the first toss:
</p>

<p>
TH, TT
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
<b>S = {HH, HT, TH, TT}</b>
</p>

<p>
This method prevents us from accidentally leaving out an outcome.
</p>
