<h2>Systematically Listing Two-Stage Outcomes</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>
<p>
For an experiment performed in two stages, we can list outcomes systematically
by fixing the first stage and then listing every possible result of the second stage.
</p>

<p>
This is important because simply guessing combinations can cause outcomes to be missed.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Two coin tosses</h3>

<p>
First toss = H.
</p>

<p>
The second toss can be H or T:
</p>

<p>
HH, HT
</p>

<p>
Now first toss = T.
</p>

<p>
The second toss can be H or T:
</p>

<p>
TH, TT
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>S = {HH, HT, TH, TT}</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2: A die followed by a coin</h3>

<p>
First stage: roll a die.
</p>

<p>
Possible first results:
</p>

<p>
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6
</p>

<p>
Second stage: toss a coin.
</p>

<p>
Possible second results:
</p>

<p>
H or T
</p>

<p>
Now attach both possible coin results to each die result:
</p>

<p>
1H, 1T<br>
2H, 2T<br>
3H, 3T<br>
4H, 4T<br>
5H, 5T<br>
6H, 6T
</p>

<p>
Therefore there are 12 possible outcomes.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Why systematic listing matters</h3>

<p>
Suppose the first stage has A or B and the second stage has 1 or 2.
</p>

<p>
Start with A:
</p>

<p>
A1, A2
</p>

<p>
Then start with B:
</p>

<p>
B1, B2
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>S = {A1, A2, B1, B2}</b>
</p>

<p>
We did not guess the combinations. We built them from the choices available at
each stage.
</p>
