<h2>Theoretical Probability from a Probability Space</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>
<p>
Once we know the complete probability space, we can find the theoretical
probability of an event when the outcomes are <b>equally likely</b>.
</p>

<h3>The rule</h3>

<p>
<b>
Probability of an event
=
Number of favourable outcomes
÷
Total number of possible outcomes
</b>
</p>

<p>
The denominator comes from the <b>complete probability space</b>.
The numerator counts the outcomes that satisfy the event we are interested in.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Rolling a die</h3>

<p>
Probability space:
</p>

<p>
<b>S = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}</b>
</p>

<p>
Suppose the event is getting a 4.
</p>

<p>
There is one favourable outcome:
</p>

<p>
<b>{4}</b>
</p>

<p>
There are six possible outcomes altogether.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(4) = 1/6
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Getting an even number</h3>

<p>
The probability space is:
</p>

<p>
<b>S = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}</b>
</p>

<p>
The even outcomes are:
</p>

<p>
<b>{2, 4, 6}</b>
</p>

<p>
There are 3 favourable outcomes and 6 total outcomes.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(even) = 3/6 = 1/2
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Two coin tosses</h3>

<p>
The probability space is:
</p>

<p>
<b>S = {HH, HT, TH, TT}</b>
</p>

<p>
Suppose we want the probability of getting two heads.
</p>

<p>
Only HH satisfies the event.
</p>

<p>
Favourable outcomes = 1.
</p>

<p>
Total outcomes = 4.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>P(two heads) = 1/4</b>
</p>

<h3>THE KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Do not choose the denominator randomly.
It comes directly from the complete probability space.
</p>

<p>
If there are 6 equally likely outcomes, the denominator is 6.
If there are 4 equally likely outcomes, the denominator is 4.
</p>
', 1),
  (261, 261, '
<h2>Impossible Events</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
An <b>impossible event</b> is an event that cannot happen in a particular experiment.
</p>

<p>
Its probability is:
</p>

<p>
<b>P(impossible event) = 0</b>
</p>

<h3>Why is its probability 0?</h3>

<p>
Probability compares:
</p>

<p>
<b>favourable outcomes</b> with <b>all possible outcomes</b>.
</p>

<p>
For an impossible event, there are <b>no favourable outcomes</b>.
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
P(event) = 0 ÷ total outcomes = 0
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Rolling a standard die</h3>

<p>
A standard die has the probability space:
</p>

<p>
S = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}
</p>

<p>
Consider the event:
</p>

<p>
<b>"Rolling a 7."</b>
</p>

<p>
There is no 7 on the die.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the number of favourable outcomes is 0.
</p>

<p>
P(7) = 0/6 = <b>0</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Tossing a coin</h3>

<p>
The probability space is:
</p>

<p>
S = {H, T}
</p>

<p>
Consider the event:
</p>

<p>
<b>"Getting both Head and Tail in one toss."</b>
</p>

<p>
One coin cannot show both results at the same time.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the event is impossible.
</p>

<p>
<b>P(event) = 0</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Selecting from numbered cards</h3>

<p>
Cards are labelled:
</p>

<p>
{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
</p>

<p>
A card is selected.
</p>

<p>
The event is selecting 8.
</p>

<p>
There is no card labelled 8.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>P(8) = 0</b>
</p>

<h3>KEY TEST</h3>

<p>
If an event <b>cannot happen</b>, its probability is exactly <b>0</b>.
</p>
', 1),
  (262, 262, '
<h2>Certain Events</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
A <b>certain event</b> is an event that must happen in a particular experiment.
</p>

<p>
Its probability is:
</p>

<p>
<b>P(certain event) = 1</b>
</p>

<h3>Why is its probability 1?</h3>

<p>
A probability compares favourable outcomes with all possible outcomes.
</p>

<p>
For a certain event, <b>every possible outcome is favourable</b>.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(event)
=
all possible outcomes ÷ all possible outcomes
=
1
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Rolling a standard die</h3>

<p>
The possible outcomes are:
</p>

<p>
S = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}
</p>

<p>
Consider the event:
</p>

<p>
<b>"Rolling a number less than 7."</b>
</p>

<p>
Every possible result is less than 7:
</p>

<p>
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6
</p>

<p>
Therefore all 6 outcomes are favourable.
</p>

<p>
P(number less than 7)
=
6/6
=
<b>1</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Tossing a coin</h3>

<p>
The possible outcomes are:
</p>

<p>
S = {H, T}
</p>

<p>
Every outcome is either Head or Tail.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the event:
</p>

<p>
<b>"Getting either Head or Tail."</b>
</p>

<p>
must happen.
</p>

<p>
P(event) = 1
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Selecting a card</h3>

<p>
A card is selected from:
</p>

<p>
{A, B, C}
</p>

<p>
The event is:
</p>

<p>
<b>"Selecting either A, B or C."</b>
</p>

<p>
Every available card satisfies the event.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(event) = 3/3 = <b>1</b>
</p>

<h3>KEY TEST</h3>

<p>
If an event <b>must happen</b>, its probability is exactly <b>1</b>.
</p>
