<h2>Interpreting Probability Values</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
A probability value tells us how strongly an event is expected to occur,
from impossible to certain.
</p>

<p>
The important values are:
</p>

<p>
<b>0</b> → impossible<br>
<b>Between 0 and 1</b> → possible but not certain<br>
<b>1</b> → certain
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Probability = 0</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
P(E) = 0
</p>

<p>
This means event E cannot happen.
</p>

<p>
For example, rolling a 10 on a standard die is impossible.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Probability = 1/2</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
P(E) = 1/2
</p>

<p>
The event is possible, but it is not guaranteed.
</p>

<p>
For a fair coin:
</p>

<p>
P(Head) = 1/2
</p>

<p>
Head can happen, but Tail can also happen.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Probability = 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
P(E) = 1
</p>

<p>
The event is certain.
</p>

<p>
For a standard die:
</p>

<p>
P(number from 1 to 6) = 1
</p>

<p>
because every possible die result is from 1 to 6.
</p>

<h3>Comparing probabilities</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
A = 0<br>
B = 1/4<br>
C = 1/2<br>
D = 1
</p>

<p>
Moving from 0 toward 1 means moving from:
</p>

<p>
<b>impossible → possible → certain</b>
</p>

<p>
The value alone gives information about the certainty of the event.
</p>
