<h2>Checking Whether a Probability Is Valid</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
A proposed probability is valid only if it satisfies:
</p>

<p>
<b>0 ≤ P(E) ≤ 1</b>
</p>

<p>
This means we check two things:
</p>

<ol>
<li>The probability must not be below 0.</li>
<li>The probability must not be above 1.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Example 1: P(E) = 0.7</h3>

<p>
Check the lower limit:
</p>

<p>
0.7 ≥ 0 ✓
</p>

<p>
Check the upper limit:
</p>

<p>
0.7 ≤ 1 ✓
</p>

<p>
Therefore 0.7 is a valid probability.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: P(E) = -0.2</h3>

<p>
Check the lower limit:
</p>

<p>
-0.2 ≥ 0 ✗
</p>

<p>
The value is below 0.
</p>

<p>
Therefore -0.2 is not a valid probability.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: P(E) = 5/4</h3>

<p>
Compare it with 1:
</p>

<p>
5/4 = 1.25
</p>

<p>
1.25 > 1
</p>

<p>
Therefore 5/4 is not a valid probability.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
A value being between 0 and 1 tells us that it <b>can</b> be a probability.
It does not automatically tell us which event has that probability.
</p>

<p>
The range rule is a validity check.
</p>
