<h2>Meaning of Mutually Exclusive Events</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
Two events are <b>mutually exclusive</b> if they cannot happen at the same
time in the same experiment.
</p>

<p>
In simple terms:
</p>

<p>
<b>If one happens, the other cannot happen in that same trial.</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Tossing one coin</h3>

<p>
The possible outcomes are:
</p>

<p>
S = {H, T}
</p>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
Event A = getting Head<br>
Event B = getting Tail
</p>

<p>
A single coin toss cannot produce Head and Tail at the same time.
</p>

<p>
Therefore A and B are <b>mutually exclusive</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Rolling one die</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
Event A = rolling 2<br>
Event B = rolling 5
</p>

<p>
A die produces one number in one roll.
It cannot show 2 and 5 as the result of the same roll.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the events are mutually exclusive.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Selecting one card</h3>

<p>
A card is selected from cards labelled:
</p>

<p>
{A, B, C, D}
</p>

<p>
Event A = selecting A<br>
Event B = selecting C
</p>

<p>
Only one card is selected.
It cannot be both A and C.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the two events are mutually exclusive.
</p>

<h3>THE CORE QUESTION</h3>

<p>
To identify mutually exclusive events, ask:
</p>

<p>
<b>"Can these two events happen together in the same trial?"</b>
</p>

<p>
If the answer is <b>no</b>, they are mutually exclusive.
