<h2>Identifying Mutually Exclusive Events</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
To determine whether two events are mutually exclusive, do not compare their
names. Compare what would have to happen in the experiment.
</p>

<p>
Ask:
</p>

<p>
<b>"Is there an outcome that belongs to both events?"</b>
</p>

<p>
If there is no such outcome, the events are mutually exclusive.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: One die</h3>

<p>
Event A = getting an even number:
</p>

<p>
A = {2, 4, 6}
</p>

<p>
Event B = getting an odd number:
</p>

<p>
B = {1, 3, 5}
</p>

<p>
There is no number appearing in both groups.
</p>

<p>
Therefore A and B are mutually exclusive.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: One die</h3>

<p>
Event A = getting a number less than 4:
</p>

<p>
A = {1, 2, 3}
</p>

<p>
Event B = getting a number greater than 4:
</p>

<p>
B = {5, 6}
</p>

<p>
There is no common outcome.
</p>

<p>
Therefore they are mutually exclusive.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: One die</h3>

<p>
Event A = getting an even number:
</p>

<p>
A = {2, 4, 6}
</p>

<p>
Event B = getting a number greater than 3:
</p>

<p>
B = {4, 5, 6}
</p>

<p>
Notice that 4 and 6 appear in both events.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the events <b>can happen together</b>.
</p>

<p>
They are <b>not mutually exclusive</b>.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
Two events do not have to be completely different in description.
What matters is whether they can share the same outcome.
