<h2>Mutually Exclusive Events from a Probability Space</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
A probability space gives us all possible outcomes.
We can use those outcomes to determine whether two events are mutually exclusive.
</p>

<p>
The key is to check whether the two events have any outcome in common.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
S = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}
</p>

<p>
Event A = {1, 3, 5}
</p>

<p>
Event B = {2, 4, 6}
</p>

<p>
Compare the two events:
</p>

<p>
A: 1, 3, 5<br>
B: 2, 4, 6
</p>

<p>
There is no common outcome.
</p>

<p>
Therefore A and B are mutually exclusive.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
S = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}
</p>

<p>
Event A = {1, 2}
</p>

<p>
Event B = {3, 4}
</p>

<p>
No outcome appears in both events.
</p>

<p>
Therefore they are mutually exclusive.
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
S = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}
</p>

<p>
Event A = {1, 2, 3}
</p>

<p>
Event B = {3, 4, 5}
</p>

<p>
The outcome 3 belongs to both events.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the events are not mutually exclusive.
</p>

<h3>THE PATTERN</h3>

<p>
<b>No common outcome → mutually exclusive.</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>At least one common outcome → not mutually exclusive.</b>
</p>
', 1),
