<h2>Mutually Exclusive Events in an Experiment</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
The easiest way to understand mutually exclusive events is to imagine carrying
out the experiment once and asking whether both events could be true for that
same result.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: One die roll</h3>

<p>
Event A: the number is even.<br>
Event B: the number is odd.
</p>

<p>
Take any possible result:
</p>

<p>
1 → odd, not even<br>
2 → even, not odd<br>
3 → odd, not even<br>
4 → even, not odd<br>
5 → odd, not even<br>
6 → even, not odd
</p>

<p>
No single result can satisfy both events.
</p>

<p>
Therefore they are mutually exclusive.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: One die roll</h3>

<p>
Event A: getting 1.<br>
Event B: getting an even number.
</p>

<p>
If the result is 1, Event A happens but Event B does not.
</p>

<p>
If the result is even, Event B happens but Event A does not.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the events cannot happen together.
They are mutually exclusive.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: One die roll</h3>

<p>
Event A: getting an even number.<br>
Event B: getting a number greater than 3.
</p>

<p>
Consider the outcome 4.
</p>

<p>
4 is even, so A happens.
</p>

<p>
4 is also greater than 3, so B happens.
</p>

<p>
Therefore both events can happen in the same trial.
</p>

<p>
They are not mutually exclusive.
</p>

<h3>THE DECISION METHOD</h3>

<p>
1. Imagine one trial of the experiment.<br>
2. Choose a possible outcome.<br>
3. Ask whether that same outcome can satisfy both events.<br>
4. If yes, they are not mutually exclusive.<br>
5. If no possible outcome can satisfy both, they are mutually exclusive.
</p>
