  (271, 271, '
<h2>Meaning of Independent Events</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
Two events are <b>independent</b> when the occurrence of one event does not
change the chance of the other event occurring.
</p>

<p>
In simple language:
</p>

<p>
<b>Knowing what happened in one event does not affect the probability of the other event.</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Tossing a coin twice</h3>

<p>
Suppose a fair coin is tossed twice.
</p>

<p>
The first toss does not physically change the coin.
The coin is still capable of producing Head or Tail on the second toss.
</p>

<p>
Before the first toss:
</p>

<p>
P(Head) = 1/2
</p>

<p>
Suppose the first toss gives Head.
</p>

<p>
The second toss still has:
</p>

<p>
P(Head) = 1/2
</p>

<p>
The first result did not change the chance of Head on the second toss.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the two tosses are independent.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Rolling a die twice</h3>

<p>
A die is rolled twice.
</p>

<p>
Suppose the first roll gives 6.
</p>

<p>
The die is rolled again.
</p>

<p>
The probability of getting 3 on the second roll is still:
</p>

<p>
<b>1/6</b>
</p>

<p>
The first roll did not change the die or remove any of its faces.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Two separate experiments</h3>

<p>
A coin is tossed and a die is rolled.
</p>

<p>
The result of the coin toss does not change the number of faces on the die.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the result of one experiment does not affect the probability of the
other experiment.
</p>

<h3>CORE IDEA</h3>

<p>
<b>Independent = one result does not affect the probability of the other.</b>
</p>
