<h2>Recognising Independent Events</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
To identify independent events, ask:
</p>

<p>
<b>"If I know the result of the first event, does that information change the probability of the second event?"</b>
</p>

<p>
If the probability remains unchanged, the events are independent.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Two coin tosses</h3>

<p>
Event A = Head on the first toss.<br>
Event B = Head on the second toss.
</p>

<p>
The first toss does not change the second toss.
</p>

<p>
Therefore A and B are independent.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Two die rolls</h3>

<p>
Event A = getting 2 on the first roll.<br>
Event B = getting 5 on the second roll.
</p>

<p>
Knowing that the first roll was 2 does not remove 5 from the die.
</p>

<p>
The probability of 5 on the second roll remains:
</p>

<p>
1/6
</p>

<p>
Therefore the events are independent.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Choosing without replacement</h3>

<p>
A bag contains red and blue balls.
One ball is selected and <b>not returned</b>.
A second ball is selected.
</p>

<p>
The first selection changes what remains in the bag.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the probability of the second selection can change.
</p>

<p>
This means the selections are generally <b>not independent</b>.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT CONTRAST</h3>

<p>
<b>With replacement:</b> the selected item is returned, so the probabilities
can remain unchanged.
</p>

<p>
<b>Without replacement:</b> the selected item is removed, so the probabilities
can change.
</p>
