<h2>Independence With Replacement</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
When an item is selected, returned to the original collection, and the
collection is restored before the next selection, the probabilities can remain
unchanged.
</p>

<p>
This is called selecting <b>with replacement</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: A bag of balls</h3>

<p>
A bag contains:
</p>

<p>
3 red balls and 2 blue balls.
</p>

<p>
Total balls:
</p>

<p>
3 + 2 = 5
</p>

<p>
Therefore initially:
</p>

<p>
P(Red) = 3/5
</p>

<p>
Suppose a red ball is selected.
</p>

<p>
The red ball is returned to the bag.
</p>

<p>
The bag again contains:
</p>

<p>
3 red + 2 blue = 5 balls.
</p>

<p>
Therefore on the second selection:
</p>

<p>
P(Red) = 3/5
</p>

<p>
The first selection did not change the probabilities.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
Suppose the first selected ball is blue.
</p>

<p>
It is returned to the bag.
</p>

<p>
The bag still contains 3 red and 2 blue balls.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(Red on second selection) = 3/5
</p>

<p>
The first colour did not change the second probability.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Why replacement matters</h3>

<p>
Before the first selection:
</p>

<p>
3 red, 2 blue.
</p>

<p>
After selecting a red ball and returning it:
</p>

<p>
3 red, 2 blue.
</p>

<p>
Nothing has changed.
</p>

<p>
That is why the second probability remains the same.
