<h2>Independence Without Replacement</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
When an item is selected and <b>not returned</b>, the collection changes.
This can change the probability of the next selection.
</p>

<p>
Therefore selections without replacement are generally <b>dependent</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Two red balls</h3>

<p>
A bag contains:
</p>

<p>
3 red balls and 2 blue balls.
</p>

<p>
Total:
</p>

<p>
5 balls.
</p>

<p>
A red ball is selected and kept outside.
</p>

<p>
Now the bag contains:
</p>

<p>
2 red balls and 2 blue balls.
</p>

<p>
Total:
</p>

<p>
4 balls.
</p>

<p>
Before the first selection:
</p>

<p>
P(Red) = 3/5
</p>

<p>
After a red ball is removed:
</p>

<p>
P(Red on second selection) = 2/4 = 1/2
</p>

<p>
The probability changed.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the selections are not independent.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: A blue ball is removed</h3>

<p>
Start with:
</p>

<p>
3 red and 2 blue.
</p>

<p>
Suppose a blue ball is selected and not replaced.
</p>

<p>
Remaining:
</p>

<p>
3 red and 1 blue.
</p>

<p>
Total = 4.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(Red on second selection) = 3/4
</p>

<p>
Originally P(Red) = 3/5.
</p>

<p>
The probability has changed.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: What caused the change?</h3>

<p>
The probability changed because the first selection removed an item.
</p>

<p>
The number of items and their composition are now different.
</p>

<p>
Therefore information about the first selection affects the probability of
the second selection.
