  (275, 275, '
<h2>Distinguishing Independent and Dependent Events</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
The difference between independent and dependent events is whether the first
event changes the probability of the second event.
</p>

<h3>Independent</h3>

<p>
The first event does <b>not</b> change the probability of the second event.
</p>

<p>
Example:
</p>

<p>
Tossing a coin twice.
</p>

<p>
The first toss does not change the second toss.
</p>

<h3>Dependent</h3>

<p>
The first event <b>does</b> change the probability of the second event.
</p>

<p>
Example:
</p>

<p>
Selecting balls from a bag without replacement.
</p>

<p>
The first ball is removed, so the contents of the bag change.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Coin tosses</h3>

<p>
A coin is tossed twice.
</p>

<p>
The first result does not change the coin.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Independent.</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Cards without replacement</h3>

<p>
A card is selected from a pack and kept aside.
A second card is then selected.
</p>

<p>
The first card is no longer available.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the possible cards and their probabilities change.
</p>

<p>
The selections are:
</p>

<p>
<b>Dependent.</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Cards with replacement</h3>

<p>
A card is selected, returned to the pack, and the pack is mixed before another
card is selected.
</p>

<p>
The original collection is restored.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the first selection does not change the second probability.
</p>

<p>
The selections are:
</p>

<p>
<b>Independent.</b>
</p>

<h3>DECISION RULE</h3>

<p>
Ask:
</p>

<p>
<b>"Does knowing the first result change the probability of the second result?"</b>
</p>

<p>
No → <b>Independent</b><br>
Yes → <b>Dependent</b>
</p>
