<h2>The Addition Law from a Probability Space</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
The addition law can be understood directly from the probability space.
We simply count the favourable outcomes belonging to either event.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A fair die is rolled.
</p>

<p>
S = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}
</p>

<p>
Event A = getting 1.
</p>

<p>
A = {1}
</p>

<p>
Event B = getting 5.
</p>

<p>
B = {5}
</p>

<p>
Together, "A or B" gives:
</p>

<p>
{1, 5}
</p>

<p>
There are 2 favourable outcomes out of 6.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(A or B) = 2/6 = 1/3
</p>

<p>
Using the addition law:
</p>

<p>
P(A) + P(B)
</p>

<p>
= 1/6 + 1/6
</p>

<p>
= 1/3
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
Event A = getting 2 or 3.
</p>

<p>
P(A) = 2/6 = 1/3
</p>

<p>
Event B = getting 5.
</p>

<p>
P(B) = 1/6
</p>

<p>
A and B are mutually exclusive.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(A or B)
= 1/3 + 1/6
</p>

<p>
Convert 1/3 to sixths:
</p>

<p>
1/3 = 2/6
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
2/6 + 1/6
= 3/6
= <b>1/2</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
Event A = getting 1, 2 or 3.
</p>

<p>
P(A) = 3/6 = 1/2
</p>

<p>
Event B = getting 4 or 5.
</p>

<p>
P(B) = 2/6 = 1/3
</p>

<p>
The events are mutually exclusive.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(A or B)
= 1/2 + 1/3
</p>

<p>
Using a common denominator of 6:
</p>

<p>
1/2 = 3/6
</p>

<p>
1/3 = 2/6
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
3/6 + 2/6
= 5/6
</p>
