<h2>Multiplying Probabilities of Independent Events</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
When two events are independent and we want the probability that <b>both</b>
events happen, we multiply their probabilities.
</p>

<p>
For independent events A and B:
</p>

<p>
<b>P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B)</b>
</p>

<h3>Why do we multiply?</h3>

<p>
Consider two coin tosses.
</p>

<p>
Suppose we want:
</p>

<p>
<b>Head on the first toss AND Head on the second toss.</b>
</p>

<p>
The first toss has:
</p>

<p>
P(H) = 1/2
</p>

<p>
The second toss also has:
</p>

<p>
P(H) = 1/2
</p>

<p>
The tosses are independent.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(H and H)
= 1/2 × 1/2
= <b>1/4</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Two die rolls</h3>

<p>
A die is rolled twice.
</p>

<p>
Find the probability of getting 6 on both rolls.
</p>

<p>
First roll:
</p>

<p>
P(6) = 1/6
</p>

<p>
Second roll:
</p>

<p>
P(6) = 1/6
</p>

<p>
The rolls are independent.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(6 and 6)
= 1/6 × 1/6
= <b>1/36</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Coin and die</h3>

<p>
A coin is tossed and a die is rolled.
</p>

<p>
Find the probability of getting Head and 4.
</p>

<p>
P(Head) = 1/2
</p>

<p>
P(4) = 1/6
</p>

<p>
The experiments are independent.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(Head and 4)
= 1/2 × 1/6
= <b>1/12</b>
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
<b>Independent + both events happen → multiply.</b>
</p>
', 1),
