<h2>Reading a Probability Tree Diagram</h2>

<p>
A <b>probability tree diagram</b> is a diagram used to show the possible
outcomes of an experiment that happens in stages.
</p>

<p>
A tree is read from <b>left to right</b>. Each level represents a stage
of the experiment.
</p>

<h3>1. What is a branch?</h3>

<p>
A <b>branch</b> represents one possible result at a particular stage.
</p>

<p>
For example, when a coin is tossed, the possible results are:
</p>

<p><b>H = Head</b></p>
<p><b>T = Tail</b></p>

<p>
Therefore the first stage has two branches:
</p>

<pre>
        H
       /
Start
       \
        T
</pre>

<h3>2. What is a path?</h3>

<p>
A <b>path</b> is a complete route from the beginning of the tree to the
end of the experiment.
</p>

<p>
If a coin is tossed twice, the first toss gives H or T.
The second toss also gives H or T.
</p>

<pre>
                 H
               /   \
              H     T
             /
Start       /
             \
              T
             / \
            H   T
</pre>

<p>
The complete outcomes are:
</p>

<p>
<b>HH, HT, TH, TT</b>
</p>

<p>
For example, the path H → T means:
</p>

<p>
<b>Head on the first toss, followed by Tail on the second toss.</b>
</p>

<h3>3. Branch versus path</h3>

<p>
Do not confuse these two ideas.
</p>

<ul>
<li>A <b>branch</b> shows one result at one stage.</li>
<li>A <b>complete path</b> shows the result of all stages.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A coin is tossed twice. List all the complete paths.
</p>

<p>
First toss: H or T.
</p>

<p>
From each first result, the second toss can be H or T.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>HH, HT, TH, TT</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A die is rolled twice. How many complete paths are possible?
</p>

<p>
The first roll has 6 possibilities:
</p>

<p>
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6
</p>

<p>
For each of these, the second roll also has 6 possibilities.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
6 × 6 = <b>36 complete paths</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A spinner has three possible results: Red, Blue and Green.
It is spun twice. How many complete paths are possible?
</p>

<p>
First spin: 3 possibilities.
</p>

<p>
Second spin: 3 possibilities for each first result.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
3 × 3 = <b>9 complete paths</b>
</p>

<p>
The important idea is that every complete path represents one complete
outcome of the two-stage experiment.
</p>
