<h2>Building a Two-Stage Probability Tree</h2>

<p>
A tree diagram should be built from the experiment itself.
Do not start by writing random branches.
</p>

<p>
For a two-stage experiment:
</p>

<ol>
<li>Identify the possible results of the first stage.</li>
<li>Draw one branch for each first-stage result.</li>
<li>From every first-stage result, draw the possible second-stage results.</li>
<li>Read each route from the start to the end.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Example: Tossing a Coin Twice</h3>

<p>
A coin is tossed twice.
</p>

<p>
First toss:
</p>

<p><b>H or T</b></p>

<p>
Second toss:
</p>

<p><b>H or T</b></p>

<p>
The structure is:
</p>

<pre>
                 H
               /   \
              H     T
             /
Start       /
             \
              T
             / \
            H   T
</pre>

<p>
The complete paths are:
</p>

<p><b>HH, HT, TH, TT</b></p>

<h3>Example 1: Die followed by a coin</h3>

<p>
A die is rolled and then a coin is tossed.
</p>

<p>
The first stage has:
</p>

<p><b>1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6</b></p>

<p>
The second stage has:
</p>

<p><b>H or T</b></p>

<p>
Therefore each die result must receive two branches:
</p>

<pre>
1 → H
  → T

2 → H
  → T

3 → H
  → T

4 → H
  → T

5 → H
  → T

6 → H
  → T
</pre>

<p>
There are:
</p>

<p>
6 × 2 = <b>12 complete paths</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Spinner spun twice</h3>

<p>
A spinner has Red, Blue and Green.
It is spun twice.
</p>

<p>
First stage:
</p>

<p><b>R, B, G</b></p>

<p>
Each first result receives:
</p>

<p><b>R, B, G</b></p>

<p>
The complete paths are:
</p>

<p>
RR, RB, RG,
</p>

<p>
BR, BB, BG,
</p>

<p>
GR, GB, GG.
</p>

<p>
There are:
</p>

<p>
3 × 3 = <b>9 paths</b>
</p>

<h3>Important check</h3>

<p>
Every possible result at the second stage must be attached to
<b>every possible first-stage result</b>.
</p>

<p>
If one branch is missing, the tree is incomplete.
</p>
