<h2>Singular and Non-Singular Matrices</h2>

<p>
A square matrix can be classified as either <b>singular</b> or
<b>non-singular</b>.
</p>

<h3>The One Rule</h3>

<p>
First calculate the determinant.
Then use this rule:
</p>

<pre>
det(A) = 0
→ Singular

det(A) ≠ 0
→ Non-singular
</pre>

<p>
So the determinant is what decides the classification.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Determine whether the matrix is singular or non-singular.</p>

<pre>
A = [ 2  4
      1  2 ]
</pre>

<h4>Step 1: Find the determinant</h4>

<pre>
det(A)
= (2 × 2) - (4 × 1)
= 4 - 4
= 0
</pre>

<h4>Step 2: Apply the rule</h4>

<pre>
det(A) = 0
<p> Therefore A is <b>singular</b>. </p> <h3>Worked Example 2</h3> <p><b>Question:</b> Determine whether the matrix is singular or non-singular.</p> <pre> B = [ 3 2 5 4 ] </pre> <h4>Find the determinant</h4> <pre> det(B) = (3 × 4) - (2 × 5) = 12 - 10 = 2 </pre> <p> Since: </p> <pre> 2 ≠ 0 </pre> <p> B is <b>non-singular</b>. </p> <h3>Worked Example 3</h3> <p><b>Question:</b> Find x if the matrix below is singular.</p> <pre> A = [ x 2 3 6 ] </pre> <p> A singular matrix has determinant zero. </p> <pre> det(A) = 0 </pre> <p> Calculate the determinant: </p> <pre> (x × 6) - (2 × 3) = 0 6x - 6 = 0 </pre> <p> Add 6 to both sides: </p> <pre> 6x = 6 </pre> <p> Divide both sides by 6: </p> <pre> x = 1 </pre> <h3>Worked Example 4</h3> <p><b>Question:</b> Find x if the matrix below is singular.</p> <pre> B = [ x 1 2 3 ] </pre> <p> Because the matrix is singular: </p> <pre> det(B) = 0 </pre> <p> Therefore: </p> <pre> (x × 3) - (1 × 2) = 0 3x - 2 = 0 </pre> <p> Add 2: </p> <pre> 3x = 2 </pre> <p> Divide by 3: </p> <pre> x = 2/3 </pre> <h3>Common Mistake</h3> <p> Do not decide that a matrix is singular just because its entries look similar. Always calculate its determinant. </p> ', 1),
