<h2>Naming a Vector from Its Initial and Terminal Points</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
When a vector is drawn between two labelled points, we can name
the vector by writing the <b>initial point first</b> and the
<b>terminal point second</b>.
</p>

<p>
The initial point is where the vector starts.
</p>

<p>
The terminal point is where the arrowhead ends.
</p>

<p>
For:
</p>

<p>
<b>A ─────────→ B</b>
</p>

<p>
A is the initial point and B is the terminal point.
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗AB</b>
</p>

<h3>DO NOT NAME IT BY POSITION</h3>

<p>
The correct name does not depend on which point is on the left
or right of the page.
</p>

<p>
What matters is the <b>direction of the arrow</b>.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>B ←──────── A</b>
</p>

<p>
The vector still moves from A to B, so it is:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗AB</b>
</p>

<h3>WHY THIS MATTERS</h3>

<p>
If you reverse the points, you reverse the vector.
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗AB ≠ ⃗BA</b>
</p>

<p>
They have the same line and the same length, but opposite directions.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>C ─────────→ D</b>
</p>

<p>
Name the vector.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Find where the arrow starts.</p>
<p>
It starts at C.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Find where the arrow ends.</p>
<p>
It ends at D.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Write start then end.</p>

<p><b>Answer: ⃗CD</b></p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>E ←──────── F</b>
</p>

<p>
Name the vector.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The arrowhead points toward E.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Therefore the vector moves from F to E.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Write F first and E second.</p>

<p><b>Answer: ⃗FE</b></p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
A vector starts at M and ends at N.
A student writes ⃗NM.
Is the student''s notation correct?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The vector starts at M.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Therefore M must be written first.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> The correct notation is ⃗MN.</p>

<p>
<b>Answer:</b> No. ⃗NM describes the opposite direction.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
<b>Always follow the arrow.</b>
</p>

<p>
Start point → end point → vector name.
</p>
