<h2>Equivalent Vectors</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
Two vectors are <b>equivalent</b> if they have the same
<b>magnitude</b> and the same <b>direction</b>.
</p>

<p>
They do not have to start at the same point.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>A ─────→ B</b>
</p>

<p>
and
</p>

<p>
<b>C ─────→ D</b>
</p>

<p>
If the arrows have equal length and point in the same direction,
then:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗AB = ⃗CD</b>
</p>

<h3>THE TWO CONDITIONS</h3>

<p>
For vectors to be equivalent:
</p>

<p><b>Condition 1:</b> Same magnitude.</p>

<p>
Their sizes must be equal.
</p>

<p><b>Condition 2:</b> Same direction.</p>

<p>
They must point the same way.
</p>

<p>
Both conditions must be satisfied.
</p>

<h3>POSITION DOES NOT MATTER</h3>

<p>
An equivalent vector can be drawn somewhere else on the page.
</p>

<p>
Moving the arrow without changing its length or direction does
not change the vector it represents.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
Vector A has magnitude 6 cm and points east.
Vector B has magnitude 6 cm and points east.
Are they equivalent?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Compare magnitudes.</p>
<p>
Both have magnitude 6 cm.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Compare directions.</p>
<p>
Both point east.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Both conditions are satisfied.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>
<p>
Yes, the vectors are equivalent.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
Vector P has magnitude 8 m and points north.
Vector Q has magnitude 8 m and points south.
Are they equivalent?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Compare magnitudes.</p>
<p>
Both have magnitude 8 m.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Compare directions.</p>
<p>
One points north and the other points south.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> The directions are different.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>
<p>
No. They are not equivalent.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
Vector X has magnitude 5 units and points east.
Vector Y has magnitude 7 units and points east.
Are they equivalent?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Compare directions.</p>
<p>
Both point east.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Compare magnitudes.</p>
<p>
5 units ≠ 7 units.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> The magnitudes are different.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>
<p>
No. They are not equivalent.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
<b>Equivalent vectors = same magnitude + same direction.</b>
</p>

<p>
Their starting positions can be different.
</p>
