<h2>Scalar Quantities</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>
<p>
A <b>scalar quantity</b> is a quantity that is described by
<b>magnitude only</b>.
</p>

<p>
<b>Magnitude</b> simply means the amount or size of something.
A scalar tells us <b>how much</b>, but it does not need to tell us
a direction.
</p>

<p>
For example, if we say:
</p>

<p><b>5 kg</b></p>

<p>
we know the amount of mass is 5 kilograms. There is no direction
attached to mass, so mass is a scalar quantity.
</p>

<h3>COMMON SCALAR QUANTITIES</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Mass — measured in kilograms (kg)</li>
  <li>Time — measured in seconds (s)</li>
  <li>Temperature — measured in degrees Celsius (°C)</li>
  <li>Distance — measured in metres (m)</li>
  <li>Speed — measured in metres per second (m/s)</li>
  <li>Volume — measured in cubic metres (m³)</li>
</ul>

<h3>HOW TO RECOGNISE A SCALAR</h3>

<p>
Ask one simple question:
</p>

<p>
<b>"Does this quantity need a direction to be completely described?"</b>
</p>

<p>
If the answer is <b>no</b>, the quantity is scalar.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
A bag has a mass of 8 kg. Is mass a scalar quantity?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the quantity.</p>
<p>The quantity is mass.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Ask whether mass needs a direction.</p>
<p>
An object can have a mass of 8 kg regardless of whether it is
moving north, south, up, or down.
</p>

<p><b>Conclusion:</b></p>
<p>
Mass is a <b>scalar quantity</b>.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
A journey takes 45 minutes. Is time a scalar quantity?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the quantity.</p>
<p>The quantity is time.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Check whether time requires direction.</p>
<p>
We only need to know that the journey lasted 45 minutes.
A direction is not required.
</p>

<p><b>Conclusion:</b></p>
<p>
Time is a <b>scalar quantity</b>.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
A car travels at 60 km/h. Is speed a scalar quantity?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the quantity.</p>
<p>The quantity is speed.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Check what information speed gives us.</p>
<p>
Speed tells us how fast the car is moving. It does not specify
which direction the car is travelling.
</p>

<p><b>Conclusion:</b></p>
<p>
Speed is a <b>scalar quantity</b>.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<p>
<b>Scalar = magnitude only.</b>
</p>
', 1),
  (318, 318, '
<h2>Vector Quantities</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
A <b>vector quantity</b> is a quantity that has both
<b>magnitude and direction</b>.
</p>

<p>
Magnitude tells us <b>how much</b>.
Direction tells us <b>which way</b>.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Vector = magnitude + direction</b>
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>20 m east</b>
</p>

<p>
The <b>20 m</b> gives the magnitude, while <b>east</b> gives
the direction.
</p>

<p>
If we only said <b>20 m</b>, the direction would be missing.
</p>

<h3>COMMON VECTOR QUANTITIES</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Displacement</li>
  <li>Velocity</li>
  <li>Force</li>
  <li>Acceleration</li>
</ul>

<h3>WHY DIRECTION MATTERS</h3>

<p>
Suppose a person moves 5 m.
</p>

<p>
That statement does not tell us which way the person moved.
They could have moved north, south, east, or west.
</p>

<p>
If we say:
</p>

<p><b>5 m east</b></p>

<p>
the movement is now completely described.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
A student walks 10 m north. Identify the magnitude and direction.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Find the magnitude.</p>
<p>
The number with the unit is <b>10 m</b>.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Find the direction.</p>
<p>
The direction is <b>north</b>.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>
<p>
Magnitude = 10 m<br>
Direction = north
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
A force of 15 N acts to the right. Identify its magnitude and direction.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Magnitude = 15 N.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Direction = right.</p>

<p>
Therefore the vector quantity is described as:
</p>

<p><b>15 N to the right</b></p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
A car has a velocity of 25 m/s east. What information makes
this a vector quantity?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The magnitude is 25 m/s.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The direction is east.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Both magnitude and direction are given.</p>

<p>
Therefore velocity is being described as a vector quantity.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
A vector must answer two questions:
</p>

<p>
<b>How much?</b> → magnitude
</p>

<p>
<b>Which way?</b> → direction
</p>
