<h2>Reading a Column Vector as Movement</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
A column vector can be read as a precise instruction for moving
from one point to another.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
4 \\
-2
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
means:
</p>

<p>
<b>4 units right, then 2 units down.</b>
</p>

<p>
The vector does not mean "4 and -2" as two unrelated numbers.
Together they describe one movement.
</p>

<h3>READING THE SIGN</h3>

<p>
For the coordinate plane:
</p>

<table>
<tr>
  <th>Component</th>
  <th>Movement</th>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>+ horizontal</td>
  <td>Right</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>- horizontal</td>
  <td>Left</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>+ vertical</td>
  <td>Up</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>- vertical</td>
  <td>Down</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
Interpret:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
6 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Horizontal component = 6.</p>
<p>
Positive means right.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Vertical component = 1.</p>
<p>
Positive means up.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>
<p>
6 units right and 1 unit up.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
Interpret:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
-5 \\
-2
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Horizontal component = -5.</p>
<p>
Therefore move 5 units left.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Vertical component = -2.</p>
<p>
Therefore move 2 units down.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>
<p>
5 units left and 2 units down.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
A vector tells a learner to move 3 units left and 7 units up.
Write the movement as a column vector.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Horizontal movement is left.</p>

<p>
Left means negative horizontal movement:
</p>

<p>
-3
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Vertical movement is up.</p>

<p>
Up means positive vertical movement:
</p>

<p>
+7
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Put horizontal first and vertical second.</p>

<p>
\[
\boxed{
\begin{pmatrix}
-3 \\
7
\end{pmatrix}}
\]
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
A column vector is a movement instruction:
</p>

<p>
<b>horizontal first → vertical second.</b>
</p>
