<h2>Representing a Geometric Vector as a Column Vector</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
A vector drawn as an arrow can be converted into a column vector
by identifying its <b>horizontal movement</b> and
<b>vertical movement</b>.
</p>

<p>
For example, suppose an arrow moves:
</p>

<p>
<b>4 units right and 3 units up.</b>
</p>

<p>
The horizontal component is:
</p>

<p><b>+4</b></p>

<p>
The vertical component is:
</p>

<p><b>+3</b></p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
\[
\boxed{
\begin{pmatrix}
4 \\
3
\end{pmatrix}}
\]
</p>

<h3>THE PROCESS</h3>

<p>
Whenever you convert a geometric vector into column form:
</p>

<ol>
  <li>Find the horizontal movement.</li>
  <li>Give it the correct sign.</li>
  <li>Find the vertical movement.</li>
  <li>Give it the correct sign.</li>
  <li>Put horizontal on top and vertical underneath.</li>
</ol>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
A vector moves 6 units right and 2 units up.
Write it as a column vector.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Horizontal movement = 6 units right.</p>

<p>
So horizontal component = +6.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Vertical movement = 2 units up.</p>

<p>
So vertical component = +2.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Write horizontal first.</p>

<p>
\[
\boxed{
\begin{pmatrix}
6 \\
2
\end{pmatrix}}
\]
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
A vector moves 5 units left and 4 units down.
Write it as a column vector.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Left means negative horizontal movement.</p>

<p>
Horizontal component = -5.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Down means negative vertical movement.</p>

<p>
Vertical component = -4.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Place the components in order.</p>

<p>
\[
\boxed{
\begin{pmatrix}
-5 \\
-4
\end{pmatrix}}
\]
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
A vector moves 3 units left and 7 units up.
Represent it as a column vector.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Horizontal movement = 3 units left.</p>

<p>
Horizontal component = -3.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Vertical movement = 7 units up.</p>

<p>
Vertical component = +7.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Put the horizontal component first.</p>

<p>
\[
\boxed{
\begin{pmatrix}
-3 \\
7
\end{pmatrix}}
\]
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Do not guess the signs.
</p>

<p>
<b>Right = +</b><br>
<b>Left = −</b><br>
<b>Up = +</b><br>
<b>Down = −</b>
</p>
', 1),
  (326, 326, '
<h2>Finding the Column Vector Between Two Points</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
When two points are given by their coordinates, we can find the
vector from one point to the other by calculating the change in
the horizontal and vertical coordinates.
</p>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
A = (2, 3)
</p>

<p>
B = (7, 5)
</p>

<p>
We want the vector from A to B.
</p>

<p>
Ask:
</p>

<p>
<b>How much did x change?</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>How much did y change?</b>
</p>

<p>
From A to B:
</p>

<p>
Horizontal change:
</p>

<p>
7 - 2 = 5
</p>

<p>
Vertical change:
</p>

<p>
5 - 3 = 2
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
\[
\vec{AB}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
5 \\
2
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<h3>THE RULE</h3>

<p>
For a vector from:
</p>

<p>
A = (x₁, y₁)
</p>

<p>
to:
</p>

<p>
B = (x₂, y₂)
</p>

<p>
calculate:
</p>

<p>
\[
\vec{AB}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
x_2-x_1 \\
y_2-y_1
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
The key idea is:
</p>

<p>
<b>destination − starting point</b>
</p>

<p>
We subtract the coordinates of where we start from the coordinates
of where we finish.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
Find the column vector from A(1, 2) to B(6, 5).
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Calculate the horizontal change.</p>

<p>
6 - 1 = 5
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Calculate the vertical change.</p>

<p>
5 - 2 = 3
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Write horizontal change above vertical change.</p>

<p>
\[
\boxed{
\vec{AB}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
5 \\
3
\end{pmatrix}}
\]
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
Find the vector from P(4, 7) to Q(1, 3).
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Horizontal change:</p>

<p>
1 - 4 = -3
</p>

<p>
So the movement is 3 units left.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Vertical change:</p>

<p>
3 - 7 = -4
</p>

<p>
So the movement is 4 units down.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Write the vector.</p>

<p>
\[
\boxed{
\vec{PQ}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
-3 \\
-4
\end{pmatrix}}
\]
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
Find the vector from C(-2, 4) to D(3, 1).
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Find the horizontal change.</p>

<p>
3 - (-2) = 3 + 2 = 5
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Find the vertical change.</p>

<p>
1 - 4 = -3
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Put the changes into column form.</p>

<p>
\[
\boxed{
\vec{CD}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
5 \\
-3
\end{pmatrix}}
\]
</p>

<p>
So the vector represents 5 units right and 3 units down.
</p>

<h3>THE SAFEST METHOD</h3>

<p>
Never try to guess the direction from the diagram.
Use:
</p>

<p>
<b>final coordinate − starting coordinate</b>
</p>

<p>
Do it separately for x and y.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
\[
\boxed{
\text{Vector}=
\begin{pmatrix}
\text{final x - starting x} \\
\text{final y - starting y}
\end{pmatrix}}
\]
</p>
', 1),
