<h2>Finding a Vector Between Two Points Using Position Vectors</h2>

<p>
Suppose A and B are two points.
</p>

<p>
We may know their position vectors:
</p>

<p>
<b>OA</b> and <b>OB</b>
</p>

<p>
We can use them to find the vector from A to B.
</p>

<p>
The rule is:
</p>

<p>
<b>AB = OB - OA</b>
</p>

<p>
In words:
</p>

<p>
<b>Vector to the destination - vector to the starting point</b>
</p>

<p>
The order matters.
For AB, we start at A and finish at B.
So we subtract A from B.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A = (2, 3)
</p>

<p>
B = (7, 6)
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
OA = (2, 3)
</p>

<p>
OB = (7, 6)
</p>

<p>
Now:
</p>

<p>
AB = OB - OA
</p>

<p>
= (7, 6) - (2, 3)
</p>

<p>
Subtract the horizontal components:
</p>

<p>
7 - 2 = 5
</p>

<p>
Subtract the vertical components:
</p>

<p>
6 - 3 = 3
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>AB = (5, 3)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A = (-4, 2)
</p>

<p>
B = (3, 8)
</p>

<p>
AB = OB - OA
</p>

<p>
= (3, 8) - (-4, 2)
</p>

<p>
Horizontal:
</p>

<p>
3 - (-4) = 3 + 4 = 7
</p>

<p>
Vertical:
</p>

<p>
8 - 2 = 6
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>AB = (7, 6)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A = (6, 5)
</p>

<p>
B = (1, -2)
</p>

<p>
AB = OB - OA
</p>

<p>
= (1, -2) - (6, 5)
</p>

<p>
Horizontal:
</p>

<p>
1 - 6 = -5
</p>

<p>
Vertical:
</p>

<p>
-2 - 5 = -7
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>AB = (-5, -7)</b>
</p>

<h3>Check the direction</h3>

<p>
The result (-5, -7) means:
</p>

<p>
5 units left and 7 units down.
</p>

<p>
That makes sense because B is left of and below A.
</p>
