  (337, 337, '
<h2>Meaning of Vector Subtraction</h2>

<p>
<b>Vector subtraction</b> means finding the movement that remains when one
vector is taken away from another vector.
</p>

<p>
For vectors, subtraction is closely connected to direction.
</p>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
<b>a = 7 units right</b>
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
<b>b = 3 units right</b>
</p>

<p>
Then:
</p>

<p>
<b>a - b = 4 units right</b>
</p>

<p>
because 3 units of the 7-unit movement are removed.
</p>

<h3>Why direction matters</h3>

<p>
Vectors are not just numbers.
They have direction.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, when subtracting vectors, we must subtract their corresponding
components while keeping their signs.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A vector represents 8 units to the right.
Another vector represents 3 units to the right.
</p>

<p>
Subtract the second from the first:
</p>

<p>
8 - 3 = 5
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Result = 5 units right.</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A vector represents 9 units upward.
Another represents 4 units upward.
</p>

<p>
9 - 4 = 5
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Result = 5 units upward.</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Consider the vectors:
</p>

<p>
<b>a = (7, 5)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>b = (2, 3)</b>
</p>

<p>
Subtract corresponding components:
</p>

<p>
Horizontal:
</p>

<p>
7 - 2 = 5
</p>

<p>
Vertical:
</p>

<p>
5 - 3 = 2
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>a - b = (5, 2)</b>
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Vector subtraction is performed component by component.
</p>

<p>
The first component is subtracted from the first component,
and the second component from the second component.
</p>
', 1),
  (338, 338, '
<h2>Negative Vectors</h2>

<p>
The <b>negative of a vector</b> has the same magnitude as the original
vector but points in the opposite direction.
</p>

<p>
If:
</p>

<p>
<b>a = (3, 5)</b>
</p>

<p>
then:
</p>

<p>
<b>-a = (-3, -5)</b>
</p>

<p>
Both components change sign.
</p>

<h3>Why do both signs change?</h3>

<p>
The first component controls horizontal direction.
The second component controls vertical direction.
</p>

<p>
Changing both signs reverses both directions.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
(3, 5)
</p>

<p>
means 3 units right and 5 units up.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
(-3, -5)
</p>

<p>
means 3 units left and 5 units down.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Given:
</p>

<p>
<b>a = (4, 2)</b>
</p>

<p>
Change the sign of each component:
</p>

<p>
4 becomes -4.
</p>

<p>
2 becomes -2.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>-a = (-4, -2)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Given:
</p>

<p>
<b>b = (-3, 6)</b>
</p>

<p>
Change both signs:
</p>

<p>
-3 becomes 3.
</p>

<p>
6 becomes -6.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>-b = (3, -6)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Given:
</p>

<p>
<b>c = (-5, -7)</b>
</p>

<p>
Reverse both directions:
</p>

<p>
-5 becomes 5.
</p>

<p>
-7 becomes 7.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>-c = (5, 7)</b>
</p>

<h3>Connection with subtraction</h3>

<p>
A vector can be subtracted by adding its negative.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>a - b = a + (-b)</b>
</p>

<p>
This idea will help us understand geometric subtraction.
', 1),
  (339, 339, '
<h2>Geometric Vector Subtraction</h2>

<p>
We can understand vector subtraction by thinking about the direction
of the second vector.
</p>

<p>
The key rule is:
</p>

<p>
<b>a - b = a + (-b)</b>
</p>

<p>
This means:
</p>

<ol>
<li>Keep vector a.</li>
<li>Reverse the direction of vector b to get -b.</li>
<li>Add a and -b.</li>
</ol>

<p>
So subtraction can be viewed as adding a vector pointing in the
opposite direction.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
<b>a = 6 units right</b>
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
<b>b = 2 units right</b>
</p>

<p>
The opposite of b is 2 units left.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
a - b
</p>

<p>
means:
</p>

<p>
6 units right + 2 units left.
</p>

<p>
The result is:
</p>

<p>
<b>4 units right.</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
<b>a = (5, 3)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>b = (2, 1)</b>
</p>

<p>
The negative of b is:
</p>

<p>
<b>-b = (-2, -1)</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
a - b = a + (-b)
</p>

<p>
= (5, 3) + (-2, -1)
</p>

<p>
= <b>(3, 2)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
<b>a = (4, 6)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>b = (-2, 3)</b>
</p>

<p>
First reverse b:
</p>

<p>
<b>-b = (2, -3)</b>
</p>

<p>
Now:
</p>

<p>
a - b
</p>

<p>
= (4, 6) + (2, -3)
</p>

<p>
= <b>(6, 3)</b>
</p>

<h3>Why this works</h3>

<p>
Subtracting a vector means moving in the opposite direction of that
vector.
</p>

<p>
That is why:
</p>

<p>
<b>a - b = a + (-b)</b>
</p>
', 1),
  (340, 340, '
<h2>Column Vector Subtraction</h2>

<p>
When vectors are written as column vectors, subtract corresponding
components.
</p>

<p>
The horizontal components are subtracted from each other.
</p>

<p>
The vertical components are also subtracted from each other.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Find:
</p>

<p>
<b>(7, 5) - (3, 2)</b>
</p>

<p>
Horizontal:
</p>

<p>
7 - 3 = 4
</p>

<p>
Vertical:
</p>

<p>
5 - 2 = 3
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>(7, 5) - (3, 2) = (4, 3)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Find:
</p>

<p>
<b>(2, 6) - (-4, 3)</b>
</p>

<p>
Horizontal:
</p>

<p>
2 - (-4) = 2 + 4 = 6
</p>

<p>
Vertical:
</p>

<p>
6 - 3 = 3
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>(2, 6) - (-4, 3) = (6, 3)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Find:
</p>

<p>
<b>(-3, 4) - (5, -2)</b>
</p>

<p>
Horizontal:
</p>

<p>
-3 - 5 = -8
</p>

<p>
Vertical:
</p>

<p>
4 - (-2) = 6
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>(-3, 4) - (5, -2) = (-8, 6)</b>
</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>
Do not subtract across components.
</p>

<p>
For example, do NOT calculate:
</p>

<p>
7 - 2 and 5 - 3.
</p>

<p>
The correct order is:
</p>

<p>
first with first,
second with second.
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
<b>(7, 5) - (3, 2) = (7 - 3, 5 - 2)</b>
</p>
